        "title": "Tactical Decision Game with John Schmitt",
        "link": "https://commoncog.com/tactical-decision-game-john-schmitt/",
        "source": "Commoncog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.651403+00:00",
        "image": "https://commoncog.com/content/images/2026/07/tactical_decision_game_john_schmitt.jpg",
        "full_content": "Aug 13 2026 9am-12pm CDT / 10pm-12am SGT. A live tactical decision game with the creator of Tactical Decision Games, John Schmitt. TDGs are an accelerated expertise training format developed for the US Marines. Schmitt has kindly agreed to run a TDG and to take questions from the membership.\nYou're receiving this email because you're a Commoncog member. Boy do I have a special announcement for you.\nLast week, NDM researcher Jared Peterson ran a successful Tactical Decision Game (TDG) session with many Commoncog members present. A TDG is an accelerated expertise training exercise that was originally designed for the US Marines. If you don't know what that is, you may scroll down; I'll explain more in the section after next.\nI'm pleased to announce that the next TDG session will be run by the inventor of TDGs himself, John Schmitt.\nEvent Details\nHere are the details:\n- Thursday, 13 August 2026, 09:00-11:00 Chicago Time (CDT) (Timezone visualisation here)\n  - New York Time (EDT): 13 August 2026, 10:00-12:00\n  - London Time (BST): 13 August 2026, 15:00-17:00\n  - Singapore Time (SGT): 13 August 2026, 22:00-00:00\n  - Sydney Time (AEST): 14 August 2026, 00:00-02:00\n- Note that this session will be two hours long. The first hour is the game itself, and the second hour is the debrief, where you can ask John any questions you might have about TDGs and how to design them for your own context.\nThis session, like the previous one, will be limited to 20 participants. (TDGs work best with 20 people max). To make sure registration is fair, registrations will be first-come-first-served, and will open on Wednesday 29 July 2026, at exactly the same starting time as the event:\n- Registrations open on Wednesday, 29 July 2026, 09:00 Chicago Time (CDT) — Timezone visualisation here\n  - New York Time (EDT): 29 July 2026, 10:00\n  - London Time (BST): 29 July 2026, 15:00\n  - Singapore Time (SGT): 29 July 2026, 22:00\n  - Sydney Time (AEST): 29 July 2026, 00:00\n- The registration link will be sent to all members by email at this time.\n- If you want to book a slot, you should probably set a calendar reminder for that time and then watch your inbox like a hawk.\nWhat are Tactical Decision Games?\nIn order to explain TDGs to you, I'm going to have to tell you a story.\nAfter the Vietnam War, the four branches of the US military that were involved — the Army, the Marine Corps, the Navy, and the Air Force — did a period of soul-searching in response to their (fairly horrible) experiences. The Army and the Marine Corps, in particular, went through the most reflection because these two branches experienced the worst of the war.\nThis led to a number of reforms. The Army's reforms led to the Air-Land Battle doctrine, which was very successful and led to decisive victories in the 1990 Gulf War. The Marine Corps, on the other hand, adopted manoeuvre warfare as its doctrine. Colonel John Schmitt, under the direction of General Al Gray, wrote Marine Corps Doctrinal Publication Number 1 (MCDP 1): Warfighting, which remains the foundational doctrine document for Marine manoeuvre warfare to this day. If you're interested in what a decentralised organisation actually looks like, I recommend reading Warfighting; it's well written and not very long.\nWhat is manoeuvre warfare? Manoeuvre warfare is a method of combat that allows a small fighting force to defeat a much larger one.\nIt does so by:\n- Setting tempo — forcing the enemy to respond to your execution speed, at a rate that leaves them disoriented\n- Focal point — attacking the enemy's critical point, at the right place and the right time\n- Surprise — which involves deception and sowing confusion, so as to attack the focal point at a decisive time, when the enemy least expects it\n- Combined arms — a combination of different unit types where counter-attack against any one type leaves the enemy vulnerable to attack from another type.\n- Flexibility — the fighting force must be able to adapt to conditions on the fly, improvising to create opportunity or take advantage of opportunities that present themselves in the chaos of the battlefield. It must also be designed to be well-rounded, self-contained and redundant, so that it may maintain different avenues of attack — either in movement, method, or some other factor. If multiple avenues of attack are constantly left open, alternatives are always available, which means opportunities can always be seized.\n- Decentralised command — each unit's orders will be accompanied by Commander's Intent — that is, what is the intention of the order? During battle, conditions may rapidly outpace the ability for central command to issue updated instructions. Therefore, units should be able to adapt to conditions to achieve the 'commander's intent' — the outcomes assigned to them without following the instructions of the order.\nIf all of this sounds like the ideas of John Boyd, that's because it is — Boyd was one of the influences.\nManoeuvre warfare requires improvisation and autonomy from lower-level units, and this creates a training challenge. How do you train soldiers to improvise on the fly in this decentralised manner? How do you help them make better decisions under the heat of battle?\nThe answer that Schmitt came up with was TDGs.\nAt its core, TDGs teach sensemaking in order to accelerate expertise. Put differently, it teaches novices to see as more experienced soldiers, so that they are able to spot tactical opportunities that they may exploit.\nIf you want to learn more about TDGs, you may listen to Schmitt's interview on the NDM podcast here. You may also find the book he wrote on TDGs, Mastering Tactics: A Tactical Decision Games Workbook here.\nIf you’d like to sign up to experience this training approach for yourself, add a reminder to your calendar for Wednesday 29 July 2026 at the time above, and wait for an email.\nWarmly,\nCedric\nPS: As a side note, I believe Schmitt is the reason the science fiction novel Ender's Game is on the Marine Corps Commandant's Professional Reading List. First, because the fighting in Ender is a perfect demonstration of manoeuvre warfare. Second, because the key phrase \"the enemy's gate is down\" is a perfect demonstration of how important sensemaking is.\n(Skip the next bit if you don't want spoilers for the book).\nIf you're not familiar with the book, Ender is a young boy who is recruited to train, with other children, in a 'battle school' — a space station orbiting around Earth. Their goal is to become good enough to bring the fight to aliens that very nearly destroyed Earth years ago. The children train in zero gravity battle rooms, where they fire stun guns at each other. The objective of the battle simulations is to have at least one member of your team enter through the opposing team's gate. Initially, everyone enters the zero-G room with their sense of 'down' carried over from the corridor outside. Ender's insight is that if you reframe the situation as \"the enemy's gate is down\", you can enter the battle room with your legs first, presenting a smaller target, plus you can use your legs as a shield, and you can come at the opposing army from angles they would never expect, giving you an advantage. Here's a clip from the movie (which, naturally, is inferior to the book):\nRecording\nOriginally published , last updated .\nIdeas and cases to accelerate business expertise\nCommoncog is a publication dedicated to the serious study of business.\nWe are businesspeople serving other businesspeople. We pursue methods to build business expertise, faster.\nYour time is valuable. We publish only what we think is useful, tested, and good.\nSign up to receive our latest writing:",
        "content": "Aug 13 2026 9am-12pm CDT / 10pm-12am SGT. A live tactical decision game with the creator of Tactical Decision Games, John Schmitt. TDGs are an accelerated expertise training format developed for the US Marines. Schmitt has kindly agreed to run a TDG and to take questions from the membership.\nYou're receiving this email because you're a Commoncog member. Boy do I have a special announcement for you...",
