        "title": "More hosepipe bans in force as drought declared - is your area affected?",
        "link": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crk661074ejo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
        "source": "BBC News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.651086+00:00",
        "image": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/c74b/live/69a57380-7fa8-11f1-a5f4-433bb9aa2e5a.jpg",
        "full_content": "More hosepipe bans in force as drought declared - is your area affected?\n- Published\nNearly three-quarters of England and all of Wales are under drought conditions, with repeated heatwaves leaving water supplies under strain.\nBoth England and Wales provisionally recorded their driest July on record. This lack of rainfall - on top of hot, dry weather in spring and early summer - has begun to take its toll on rivers, crops and nature.\nWessex Water has become the latest water provider to announce a hosepipe ban - the company's first for 50 years - taking the total number of people under such a measure to nearly 30 million.\nSo how is your area doing and are you in a drought?\nThere is no single definition of drought, but it is characterised by a period of low rainfall. As the ground dries out, crops and vegetation can begin to struggle.\nIf dry conditions persist, rivers, reservoirs and groundwater levels can also fall, putting pressure on water supplies.\nEnvironmental bodies in each UK nation decide whether to declare drought or water scarcity by assessing current water resources alongside weather forecasts.\nBoth drought and water scarcity can lead to hosepipe bans, if they are not already in place.\nWhile drought is driven by prolonged dry weather, water scarcity occurs when demand for water outstrips the available supply. This summer, restrictions have been introduced in some areas to help manage high demand.\nBut while recent months have seen temperatures regularly exceed 35C, winter was much wetter than usual.\nIt has raised questions about how effectively water resources are being managed, and whether the UK is prepared for the drier summers expected with climate change.\nMost of England and all of Wales in drought\nSo far, ten UK water companies have announced hosepipe bans. This means hosepipes cannot be used for watering gardens, washing vehicles and windows, and filling pools.\nNine companies are spread across central, southern and eastern parts of England, and one is in Wales.\nIn England, nearly three-quarters of the country is now in drought, according to the Environment Agency (EA). Northern parts of England are in \"prolonged dry weather\" - the category below drought.\nElsewhere, the whole of Wales is in drought, with Natural Resources Wales saying sustained high temperatures were causing the condition of the natural environment to \"deteriorate\".\nThere are no official droughts currently in Northern Ireland.\nScotland does not declare droughts but monitors \"water scarcity\".\nWhile conditions have improved in some parts of Scotland following recent rainfall, some eastern areas remain in the two most extreme categories of \"moderate\" or \"significant\" scarcity, according to the Scottish Environment Protection Agency.\nRivers, reservoirs and groundwater - how are supplies?\nOur three main sources of water are rivers, reservoirs and from deep underground.\nAlmost all river flows in England and Wales were classed as \"below normal\", \"notably low\" or \"exceptionally low\" in July, according to the UK Centre for Ecology & Hydrology (UKCEH).\n\"This is the third time in the last five years that drought conditions have intensified rapidly following exceptional spring and summer dryness,\" said Steve Turner, a hydrologist at the UKCEH.\nHe added that low river flows are likely to persist well into the autumn.\nEngland's reservoirs are some way below average levels for the time of year. Wales' reservoirs are also declining, but Dŵr Cymru Welsh Water has said its water resource position is \"relatively strong\".\nReservoir levels in Northern Ireland and Scotland are close to average, according to the latest available data.\nSouth-east England relies more heavily on groundwater than reservoirs.\nGroundwater originates as rainfall and is naturally stored beneath the surface in the pore spaces and fractures in rocks.\nGroundwater levels are generally declining following the prolonged dry weather, but almost half of monitored groundwater sites recorded levels considered \"normal\" or higher in July.\nWater can flow more slowly through some rock types than others, sometimes taking years to fully respond to current conditions.\nThese groundwater stores \"respond more slowly to changes in the climate than rivers, which is why they provide a useful buffer during periods of drought\", said Prof Alan MacDonald of the British Geological Survey.\nIt is why groundwater droughts in the south of England generally take a longer time to develop but can be longer-lasting if they do occur.\nIs climate change to blame?\nDroughts are complex phenomena, driven by a mix of natural and human causes.\nThe Met Office expects the UK to experience drier summers on average in future as the world warms, though there has been no clear trend so far.\nBut rising temperatures can play a more direct role by drawing extra moisture from the soil via evaporation.\n\"A warmer atmosphere is thirstier for moisture and this can mean water in the soil, rivers and reservoirs are depleted more effectively, leading to more rapidly onsetting droughts, heatwaves and wildfires,\" said Richard Allan, professor of climate science at the University of Reading.\nBut there are other factors that determine whether dry conditions lead to water shortages, including how we use water.\nIn a landmark review into the water sector of England and Wales, water companies were criticised for historic underinvestment in water infrastructure.\nAs part of plans to address water shortages, the government and water companies are planning nine new reservoirs for England by 2050, in addition to one under construction at Havant Thicket in Hampshire.\nBut the EA has warned that measures to tackle water leaks and control water demand - potentially including hosepipe bans - may be needed in England too.\nWater companies in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland - which are all public bodies or non-profit - also said they were taking steps to secure future supplies.\nGet in touch\nTell us how you're affected by the hosepipe ban.\n- A really simple guide to climate change \n  - Published14 January\n- How climate change worsens heatwaves, droughts, wildfires and floods \n  - Published5 November 2025\nSign up for our Future Earth newsletter to keep up with the latest climate and environment stories with the BBC's Justin Rowlatt. Outside the UK? Sign up to our international newsletter here.",
        "content": "More hosepipe bans in force as drought declared - is your area affected?\n- Published\nNearly three-quarters of England and all of Wales are under drought conditions, with repeated heatwaves leaving water supplies under strain.\nBoth England and Wales provisionally recorded their driest July on record. This lack of rainfall - on top of hot, dry weather in spring and early summer - has begun to take i...",
