        "title": "'Days of the farmers' union dictating policy are over' - Muir",
        "link": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdj0pv2m812o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
        "source": "BBC News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.418824+00:00",
        "image": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/a261/live/4e1c1f00-97fa-11f1-9577-b1309b6ea3af.jpg",
        "full_content": "'Days of the farmers' union dictating policy are over' - Muir\n- Published\nThe Agriculture Minister Andrew Muir has said \"the days of the Ulster Farmers' Union (UFU) dictating government policy are over\".\nIt comes after the UFU extended a vote of no confidence in the department to include the minister, the permanent secretary and the chief vet, over how a cull of 51 cattle was carried out in County Tyrone.\nThe cows were shot in June, leading to complaints about the manner of the cull which was carried out within earshot of members of the public.\nThe UFU President John McLenaghan had said there were concerns about the independence of a Department of Agriculture, Environment and Rural Affairs (Daera) review into the incident.\n'Extremely challenging'\nMuir said he would continue to engage with the UFU and had spoken with its president on Friday morning.\nHe added that the situation he had inherited in the department was \"extremely challenging\", with issues building up \"due to wrong government policy previously\".\nHe said he was \"arguably the first minister in the history of this state that is not beholden to one vested interest\".\nMuir described some of the speeches at a protest outside Stormont on Monday as \"pure electioneering\" and called on his fellow politicians to \"calm it down\".\nDeath threats\nEarlier this week, Muir said a death threat had been made against him. He also received one in June.\nOn Friday, he said \"the police will be coming looking\" for those who threatened or abused anyone in public life.\nIn a statement to BBC News NI, the Police Service of Northern Ireland (PSNI) said it does \"not discuss the security of individuals and no inference should be drawn from this\".\n\"However, we will continue to do all we can to ensure that all of our politicians are supported and that they can undertake their role free from fear and intimidation,\" Insp Roberts said.\nReaction from civil servants' union\nThe UFU also called for Daera's Permanent Secretary Katrina Godfrey and the Chief Veterinary Officer Brian Dooher to resign.\nIn response, the senior civil servants' union the FDA said it was \"entirely inappropriate\" to call for the resignation of officials who were \"duty-bound to serve ministers\" and \"could not defend themselves publicly\".\nThe minister has previously said there was no reasonable alternative to the cattle being euthanised on site and has insisted it was a last resort as officials had to act on a court order.\nAn external review of the cattle cull was announced by Daera earlier this month, but the UFU's Executive Committee met on Thursday night.\nIn a statement on Friday the UFU said it \"does not have the confidence in the independence of the review as presently constituted, including the scope of the Terms of Reference and appointment of a single person to conduct it.\n\"The UFU has consistently maintained that an investigation into an incident of this seriousness must be demonstrably independent, robust and capable of commanding the confidence of farm families and the wider public.\"\nJohn McLenaghan said calling for resignations was not a decision his union had taken \"lightly\".\nHowever he added that confidence had been \"severely damaged\".\nHe said the most recent event is \"just one of many instances where farmers feel that the Department has lost touch with farmers\".\n\"We no longer have confidence that the department can deliver.\"\nA Daera spokesperson said they and the minister were aware of the decision.\n\"An external independent review is already underway to review the action taken by Department officials in response to the Deprivation Order in Fivemiletown,\" they continued.\n\"There are many strategic issues and challenges for agriculture.\n\"It is vital we face up to those challenges together to restore our environment and ensure a sustainable future for agriculture\".\nResponding to the vote against the two named senior officials, the FDA union's Robert Murtagh said: \"This kind of performative stunt, naming and targeting officials, does not aid understanding and undermines the need for constructive dialogue.\n\"The UFU's action appears also to be seeking to pre-empt and to influence the independent review already now in train.\n\"It should be awaiting its findings and recommendations before jumping to respond to voices who do not care about public service or indeed public servants.\"\nWhat happened in Fivemiletown?\nWarning: This article contains an image of dead animals that some may find distressing\nAnimal welfare inspections on the farm dating back to 2022 identified serious concerns, including 33 animal carcasses, feed contaminated with urine and faeces, and emaciated animals.\nThe farmer was found guilty of a number of animal welfare offences in May and disqualified from keeping animals for life.\nThe offences included:\n- Thirteen charges of failure to dispose of an animal carcass\n- One charge of failure to comply with an Animal By-Products notice\n- Six charges of causing unnecessary suffering to an animal\n- One charge of failure to produce medicine records\nThe court issued a deprivation order on 8 June - that the farmer must have the animals removed from his ownership.\nAs is practice, he was given two weeks to appeal the sentence, or to transfer, sell or give away his stock.\nHe was then given an extra week.\nOn 29 June, when the extended grace period had lapsed, the departmental humane slaughter team arrived at the farm to begin destruction of the remaining 51 animals by shooting them.\nThe operation was completed on 30 June.\nSome members of the public witnessed the operation in one of the three areas across the farm that the animals were in.\nQuestions about how the operation was conducted were first raised by a local councillor, then concerns were expressed by politicians and farming leaders.\nThe AERA committee was recalled to discuss the incident, and quiz the minister and veterinary officials.\nWhat was the Stormont committee told?\nThe committee heard that officials had been working with the farmer after \"numerous\" inspections, beginning in 2022.\nThe agriculture minister Andrew Muir said those had revealed a \"grotesque\" situation and \"a large number of animals were caused unnecessary suffering\", leading to the deprivation order.\n\"That order legally required the department to deprive the individual of any livestock.\n\"The department had no choice.\n\"And so my officials took the necessary steps to fulfil this legal obligation.\"\nIt emerged that a total of 21 animals had been removed during the grace period, but 51 remained on the farm.\nOfficials said animals in two of the areas on the farm were disposed of by 13:30 on 29 June.\nThe slaughter operation in the final area was stopped when a local farmer offered to buy some of the remaining cattle.\nBut the animals could not be rounded up, and escaped onto the road.\nThey were eventually herded back and a vet recommended they be shot.\nOne remaining animal was disposed of on 30 June.\nThe carcasses were removed later that day.\nQuestions have been asked about the weapons used, the amount of ammunition, and why slaughter in situ was chosen as the method of disposal.\nDeputy Chief Veterinary Officer Gemma Daly described the cattle as \"semi-feral\" and said staff had expressed concerns over the years about how to handle the animals without causing them stress, or risking injury to themselves.\nSenior Principal Veterinary Officer Elsie McGarvey said the department was \"technically\" potentially in breach of the court order by extending the grace period.\nShe added that approximately half of the animals were not suitable for the food chain and others were too young.\nAnd she said officials were \"always prepared\" that the farmer would be disqualified and they \"knew\" removing the animals would present \"significant issues\".\nHow will the review be carried out?\nBefore the committee met, the minister announced that an independent external review, external would be conducted.\nIt will be led by Dr Martin Blake, a former Chief Veterinary Officer for Ireland.\nHis work will include looking at the decision-making, risk assessments and communication around the operation.\nHe will make recommendations, based on his examination of the records and any interviews he carries out.\nMuir said Dr Blake's review would be \"thorough but not prolonged\", and told the committee it would be complete \"by early autumn\".\nThe UFU has called for the review to be conducted by a panel of experts, rather than an individual.\nAn uneasy relationship - analysis\nAgriculture and environment are uncomfortable but inseparable bedfellows under the department.\nAnd the relationship between Daera and the UFU has been fractious in the past.\nThe UFU's Executive passed a vote of no confidence in the department - not the minister - in October last year.\nThat came after an outcry over the initial consultation document for the Nutrients Action Programme.\nIt has since been revised by a stakeholder group of farmers, agri-food representatives, conservationists and environmental groups.\nBut the size of the protest on Monday about this incident shows farmers are not happy.\nThe findings of Dr Blake's review will be closely scrutinised.\nThe Democratic Unionist Party (DUP) has tabled a motion of no confidence in Andrew Muir which requires 30 signatures to make it to the floor of the Assembly.\nBut any change at the top of the department would have to come from the minister's party - or the minister himself.\nAnd there are just seven months left for this minister to get a substantial number of significant policies past the Executive.\nWhat is a deprivation order?\nA deprivation order may be issued by a court when a person is convicted of animal welfare charges.\nIt requires that person to be deprived of ownership of the animal - to have it taken away - and for the animal to be disposed of.\nThe court may specify how that disposal will happen, or it may delegate that decision to a person appointed to carry out the order.\nThe legislation , externalstates that \"disposal\" includes destroying an animal.\n  - Published10 August\n  - Published2 August",
        "content": "'Days of the farmers' union dictating policy are over' - Muir\n- Published\nThe Agriculture Minister Andrew Muir has said \"the days of the Ulster Farmers' Union (UFU) dictating government policy are over\".\nIt comes after the UFU extended a vote of no confidence in the department to include the minister, the permanent secretary and the chief vet, over how a cull of 51 cattle was carried out in County...",
