        "full_content": "We believe everyone should be able to make financial decisions with confidence. While we don't cover every company or financial product on the market, we work hard to share a wide range of offers and objective editorial perspectives.\nSo how do we make money? Our partners compensate us for advertisements that appear on our site. This compensation helps us provide tools and services - like free credit score access and monitoring. With the exception of mortgage, home equity and other home-lending products or services, partner compensation is one of several factors that may affect which products we highlight and where they appear on our site. Other factors include your credit profile, product availability and proprietary website methodologies.\nHowever, these factors do not influence our editors' opinions or ratings, which are based on independent research and analysis. Our partners cannot pay us to guarantee favorable reviews. Here is a list of our partners.\nThe Best Award Travel Search Tools\nThese tools can help you find the perfect redemption for your airline, hotel and credit card points.\nCraig Joseph is a NerdWallet credit cards and travel rewards expert. He has degrees in geology from West Virginia University and oceanography from Oregon State University and has published in academic journals, newspapers and blogs. Craig is passionate about personal finance and wants to enhance the financial literacy of everyone he meets. He'll probably also try to convince you why rocks are cool.\nClaire Tsosie is a managing editor for the Travel Rewards team at NerdWallet. She started her career on the credit cards team as a writer, then worked as an editor on New Markets. Her work has been featured by Forbes, USA Today and The Associated Press.\nPublished in\nPublished\nHow is this page expert verified?\nNerdWallet's content is fact-checked for accuracy, timeliness and relevance. It undergoes a thorough review process involving writers and editors to ensure the information is as clear and complete as possible.\nSearching for the perfect way to use your miles and points can seem like a part-time job. But the right award travel search tool can do the hard work for you.\nChoose one that’s aligned with your particular redemption. One tool may be best for finding a specific flight on a particular date, while another tool will work better if your dates are flexible or you want recommendations on where your points can take you.\nHere are NerdWallet’s top picks.\nHow award search tools workHow award search tools work\nDespite their individual differences, most award search tools function like Kayak or Google Flights, only for award travel. You select the airline, hotel or credit card points you want to redeem, input your travel details, and they show you the available options.\nSome tools use live search data, while others use cached data. Cached data can range from hours to days or weeks old. For that reason, always check for award availability directly with the airline or hotel before you transfer points.\n🤓Nerdy Tip\nNo award search tool is perfect. Be sure to confirm award availability with the airline or hotel website before you transfer points from your credit card to a loyalty program.\nSubscribe to our free TravelNerd newsletter for inspiration, tips and money-saving strategies, delivered straight to your inbox.\nBy signing up, you will receive newsletters and promotional content and agree to our Terms of Use and acknowledge the data practices in our Privacy Policy. You may unsubscribe at any time.\nPoints Path: Best overall airline search tool\nPoints Path Pro - 1 year\nLimited-Time Deal: Get 15% off your first year of Pro\nPoints Path is a browser extension and app that shows airline award prices next to cash fares on Google Flights. It’s fast, accurate and provides a simple search and results interface to understand if you’re better off using points or cash for each flight based on the Points Path valuation for each airline’s rewards.\nWe picked Points Path as the best overall airline search tool because its features and search function allow people to find award flights using the same process they typically use when searching for cash flights. There are no extra steps or complicated features to navigate, and the interface is easy to understand.\nThe free version provides access to five major domestic airlines, while the Pro version provides access to 12 additional international airlines. Features include everything that typically comes with Google Flights, including the ability to search across multiple origin and destination airports and a chart showing historical pricing trends, so you know if it’s a good time to book.\nAll searches are live and there’s no cached discovery. That means you’ll know the results can be booked. One drawback: It’s limited to the search capabilities of Google Flights and users can’t scan results over a broad set of dates or queries.\nWith the tool, you can:\nFilter for pricing that includes cardholder discounts for both United Airlines and Delta Air Lines (for example, the 15% off award flights for eligible Delta cardholders).\nFilter out basic economy results, mixed-cabin itineraries and award programs that may not be relevant to you.\nView active credit card transfer bonuses, and see where you can get the best value from your transferable rewards.\nPoints Path is also the only award search tool to actively tell you if an airline program is not online and won’t show in the search results. Search tools sometimes lose temporary access to individual airlines, so this feature will let you know why a potential redemption you’d expect to see isn’t showing up.\nCreate alerts to be notified when the best seats are released.\nBest for\nDynamic Pricing\nDisplays dynamically priced flight awards only when they have reasonable pricing.\nFree version\nUnlimited Alerts\nCreate alerts to be notified when the best seats are released.\nBest for\nDynamic Pricing\nDisplays dynamically priced flight awards only when they have reasonable pricing.\nPros\nFree & Pro Versions.\nUnlimited Alerts.\nBook with points & miles on over 20 mileage programs and hundreds of airlines.\nCons\nUses of cached rather than real-time data.\nPros\nFree & Pro Versions.\nUnlimited Alerts.\nBook with points & miles on over 20 mileage programs and hundreds of airlines.\nCons\nUses of cached rather than real-time data.\nIf your travel dates are flexible, Seats.aero is probably the most powerful tool for you. You can search for premium cabin award space across a wide range of dates and airports for specific aspirational redemptions like Japan Airlines first class, Qatar Airways Qsuites and Etihad apartments. Pro users can search over an entire year for availability, and all users can filter by regions, such as North America and Europe.\nWhile Seats.aero is powerful, it’s not very beginner-friendly. That’s why Points Path is ranked as our better overall tool, even though Seats.aero covers more airline programs (25). In NerdWallet’s hands-on testing, we found that the user interface wasn’t always intuitive. To make sense of the results, you’ll need to have more details about each award program, especially around how partner award availability is handled. Additionally, text or email alerts are removed after they’re triggered, so if you don’t like the results from the first alert, you have to set it up again. But there’s also no limit on the number of alerts Pro users can set.\nSimilar to Points Path, Seats.aero has a Chrome extension that shows award pricing inside Google Flights. Even better, the extension also works in Chase’s travel portal. That’s huge, as you can immediately see whether you’ll get a better value for Chase Ultimate Rewards® by booking through the portal or transferring points to a partner airline.\nSeats.aero generally displays cached search results and only uses live results for Pro users performing specific origin-and-destination searches. For that reason, you’ll want to confirm award availability with the specific airline website before transferring credit card points.\nDaydream Explorer feature helps generate trip ideas.\nAlerts are easy to set up.\nCons\nMulti-city searches are limited to two flight segments.\nPremium product requires annual membership.\nSometimes displays \"phantom award space,\" where spaces appear to be bookable but aren't.\nPros\nMakes it easy to compare cash and points rates.\nAllows you to search for hotel award stays.\nDaydream Explorer feature helps generate trip ideas.\nAlerts are easy to set up.\nCons\nMulti-city searches are limited to two flight segments.\nPremium product requires annual membership.\nSometimes displays \"phantom award space,\" where spaces appear to be bookable but aren't.\nPointsYeah is a great all-around tool, but the most notable aspect is its map-based “Daydream Explorer” feature. It’s great if you have a stack of travel rewards and want to see different places you can use them for both flights and hotels.\nFor flights, the explorer tool can search over a 60-day window using useful filters, including:\nCabin type (economy, business or first).\nThe type of airline or credit card points you want to use.\nSeveral trip themes, such as beach, mountains, golf or scuba diving.\nYou can search between up to three arrival and three departure airports, continents or regions.\nFor hotels, you can search over a 13-month period. The filters allow you to find properties to use free night certificates and Fine Hotels and Resorts (FHR) credits from the American Express Platinum Card® (terms apply; see rates and fees). (There isn’t a filter for using The Edit credits from the Chase Sapphire Reserve®, though.)\nNote that Daydream Explorer uses cached discovery, meaning the results aren’t live. However, PointsYeah also supports fast, live searches through its standard award search engine.\nPoint.me recently refreshed its interface and increased the speed of searches. Results offer integrated booking instructions for transferring points from your credit card to the airline loyalty program, which may be helpful if you’re starting out in the world of miles and points.\nPoint.me offers limited complimentary search access to American Express and Capital One Business card holders. However, you can only search for award availability from airlines that are transfer partners of those rewards programs.\nWhen you create an account, you'll get free access to all of Gondola's features.\nLoyalty program rewards\nEarn 3% to 7% back on cash bookings\nMembers earn Gondola Cash on cash bookings, with rates based on their loyalty status.\nPrice\nFree\nWhen you create an account, you'll get free access to all of Gondola's features.\nLoyalty program rewards\nEarn 3% to 7% back on cash bookings\nMembers earn Gondola Cash on cash bookings, with rates based on their loyalty status.\nPros\nSearches are fast and accurate.\nIt’s great for trip planning.\nIt helps you find the best value for money or points.\nPrice drop alerts can save you money or points.\nEarn extra rewards with Gondola status.\nCons\nThere are no price or award availability alerts.\nYou may have data privacy concerns.\nPros\nSearches are fast and accurate.\nIt’s great for trip planning.\nIt helps you find the best value for money or points.\nPrice drop alerts can save you money or points.\nEarn extra rewards with Gondola status.\nCons\nThere are no price or award availability alerts.\nYou may have data privacy concerns.\nGondola makes it easy to compare cash and award prices across brands and to stack rewards when booking with cash.\nSay you’re trying to decide between redeeming Hyatt points and Marriott points. Gondola can show you the cash and points costs for properties in both chains, plus color-coded redemption values. That makes it easy to scan the results for the best deals.\nGondola includes many of the features you’d expect from a strong travel search platform, like alerts when cash or points prices fall, fast search results and useful historical pricing data for individual hotels. You can also sort results by redemption value, making them easier to scan than other tools such as PointsYeah and Rooms.aero.\nBeyond credit card and hotel loyalty rewards, Gondola has its own rewards program called Gondola Cash, which gives you 3% to 7% back on future cash bookings depending on your status with Gondola.\nTo view rates and fees of the American Express Platinum Card®, see this page.",
