        "title": "Live: Butler slams One Nation plan to slash tobacco excise as 'cheap headline'",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/federal-politics-live-blog-butler-slams-one-nation-plan/107052772",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417795+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/386fc51378805e036ae1e25aae5328a7?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=900&cropW=1600&xPos=0&yPos=0&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "live\nFederal politics live: Butler slams One Nation plan to slash tobacco excise as 'cheap headline'\nHealth Minister Mark Butler has slammed a One Nation plan to slash the tobacco excise, describing it as nothing but a \"cheap headline\".\nFollow all the updates in our live blog.\nSubmit a comment or question\nLive updates\nNew: Filters\nChoose what information you see below by using filters\n📸 Thai PM Anutin Charnvirakul welcomed to parliament\nWe're expecting a joint media statement from Mr Charnvirakul and Anthony Albanese later this morning.\nRight balance struck on gambling ads, Taylor says\nOpposition Leader Angus Taylor says they have \"struck the right balance\" on the gambling advertising reforms, after the legislation passed the lower house yesterday, following a flurry of negotiations with the government. \nThe agreement will lead to the establishment of a new register where Australians can opt out of betting company ads online.\nTwo Coalition MPs, Andrew Wallace and Pat Conaghan, opposed the bill, saying it didn't go far enough.\nTaylor acknowledged it was an \"emotional\" issue but said the deal delivered appropriate protections for the vulnerable.\n\"I'm very pleased that we have got bipartisan support for this legislation now,\" he said.\n\"It'll get the balance right between people having a bet, and they should be able to have a bet and, at the same time, making sure there's protection for the most vulnerable.\"\nTaylor dons the lyrca, signals own policy on tobacco excise 'in the not so distant future'\nOpposition Leader Angus Taylor is donning the lycra in Canberra today to launch the annual \"Pollie Pedal\", a charity bike ride to raise funds for veterans.\nBut of course, he can't escape questions from reporters (even when in lycra). After One Nation put forward the idea to cut the tobacco excise, Taylor was asked if the Coalition would do the same.\n\"There's no question at the moment. We're seeing illegal tobacco flooding this country and it's funding organised crime in the country,\" he said.\n\"There needs to be action on this and we'll have more to say on this in the not too distant future.\"\nFears NDIS overhaul will have devastating consequences\nOne of Australia's peak advocacy organisations for people with disability has warned the overhaul of the NDIS will cost lives.\nUnder the reforms, which passed the Senate last night, from January 1, 2028, access to NDIS will be based on a standardised assessment of functional capacity, and current participants will be reassessed.\nAs a result of the changes, hundreds of thousands of people are expected to be removed or prevented from accessing the scheme, with many expected to rely on yet-to-be-established foundational supports.\nPeople with Disability Australia interim president and NDIS participant Jarrod Sandell-Hay says he is deeply concerned many people will be forced to exit the scheme with no safety net.\n\"Lives will be lost because of this bill. And they know that. And so I'm not quite sure and the community is not quite sure why they're still pushing ahead,\" Sandell-Hay tells ABC's AM.\n\"Whatever outcome they're looking for, it's not going to be what they want.\"\nHealth Minister Mark Butler yesterday acknowledged work \"must begin quickly\" on the alternative supports to be run by the states.\n\"We've got a lot of work to do to develop the equivalent systems, effectively, for kids over the age of nine and for other adults who might be impacted by these eligibility arrangements,\" Butler said.\n\"That's a tonne of work, but it is incredibly important.\"\nKey Event\nWallace on the 'once-in-a-career opportunity to take a stand' on gambling ads\nLiberal MP Andrew Wallace has been in parliament for a decade. Yesterday marked the first time he's crossed the floor on a vote.\nHe did so to protest against the gambling advertising reforms, which he says don't go far enough.\n\"I want to see an end to that nexus between sport and gambling. I'm sick to death of seeing those gambling ads, particularly around sports,\" he tells ABC's Radio National Breakfast.\n\"And we're seeing a whole young cohort of kids growing up thinking gambling is part of sport and it's not. I fundamentally believe this was probably a once-in-a-career opportunity for me to make a stand.\n\"And I don't think the bill went far enough.\"\nWallace acknowledged the opposition \"made a bad bill better\", but it wasn't enough.\n\"At the end of the day ... I've got to look at myself in the mirror and I've got to look at my constituents in the face and say I did what I thought was right.\"\nHe says he won't stop in his fight, and will continue to lobby for the laws to be strengthened even if he's not in parliament when the statutory review takes place in three years' time.\nFight with One Nation getting 'dirty and nasty': Hastie\nAndrew Hastie was also asked about the ongoing battle with One Nation over in the West.\nA state by-election is currently taking place in Secret Harbour, which sits within Hastie's federal seat of Canning. The seat is held by Labor but is facing a strong challenge from One Nation. The minor party has said it plans to challenge Hastie at the next election.\nThe Liberal frontbencher says the fight has got pretty \"dirty and nasty\".\n\"The way things have been done online over the last couple of months, I think it's pretty obvious,\" he says, adding he'd had some \"pretty strong messages\" sent to himself and his staff.\n\"That's why I've been identified for upgrades for security, for my family home, and my office. That's not going to deter me.\"\nSo how should the Liberals be handling the threat of One Nation? Hastie urges the party not to get distracted.\n\"It's not a thought bubble race on X with Pauline Hanson. We want to make sure that our policies are costed and modeled and are responsible,\" he says.\n\"It's very easy to get a sugar hit in the headlines but we're doing the hard work.\"\nHe points to work underway within the Coalition on the tobacco excise.\n'Politics is the art of the possible': Hastie on gambling ad deal\nLiberal frontbencher Andrew Hastie was one of several MPs to sign a letter calling for the government to strengthen its gambling ad reforms.\nSo is he disappointed where the bill landed, considering it doesn't include a national regulator? No, he tells ABC's Radio National Breakfast.\n\"I think we've landed in the right spot. Politics is the art of the possible, and we wanted to improve the government's bill, and we've done a lot of work,\" he says.\n\"There's always more you can do, but in the end, I think we're trying to work in the interests of the Australian people and we've made some good progress.\"\nHe says he respected his colleague, Andrew Wallace, and his decision to cross the floor yesterday to vote against the reform. Wallace did so because he didn't think the legislation went far enough.\nNo plans to lift super guarantee to 15 per cent, Mulino says\nAssistant Treasurer Daniel Mulino says there are no plans to lift the superannuation guarantee from 12 per cent to 15 per cent.\n\"Getting it to 12 per cent was the work of Labor governments over a very long period of time. And I might say, in the face of opposition, it was fought tooth and nail every step of the way by the opposition,\" he says.\nGovernment to crack down on super switching schemes\nAssistant Treasurer Daniel Mulino will appear at the National Press Club today, where he'll announce a crackdown on super switching schemes.\nUnlicensed telemarketers who cold call and make unsolicited approaches to convince people to switch their super will be banned under the long-awaited reforms.\nThe reforms follow the collapse of the Shield and First Guardian funds, which saw 12,000 people lose more than $1 billion in retirement savings and exposed deep flaws in the regulation of Australia's almost $4.5 trillion retirement savings pool.\nMulino tells ABC's Radio National Breakfast that the scale of that collapse was \"shocking\". \n\"But what was also increasingly clear was the predatory and inappropriate behaviour which led to those collapses, generators reaching out to these people in unsolicited ways, [and] undertaking highly manipulative interactions to convince them that their super products were not performing well for them.\"\nGovernment trying to claim victory by doing 'bare minimum' on gambling ads: Pocock\nDavid Pocock is not too happy about where the government's landed on gambling advertising, saying the legislation \"fails on so many different levels\".\nThe independent senator says the government was handed a blueprint by late Labor MP Peta Murphy, who chaired the committee that authored the landmark \"you win some, you lose more\" report. That inquiry recommended a ban on inducements and online advertising.\nSpeaking with ABC's News Breakfast, Pocock said the legislation before the parliament only bans the \"worst types of inducements\" and doesn't even touch the idea of a national regulator.\n\"The system is broken and it's no wonder we are the biggest losers in the world per capita. And now the government is trying to claim this huge victory for just doing the bare minimum,\" he says.\nRight balance struck on gambling ad changes, Henderson says\nShadow Communications Minister Sarah Henderson is pleased with the deal the opposition has struck with the government on gambling advertising.\nThe deal resulted in the legislation passing the lower house yesterday. It's expected to be signed off by the Senate before the end of the week.\nNot everyone within the Coalition was on board though, with Andrew Wallace and Pat Conaghan crossing the floor.\nHenderson told ABC's Radio National Breakfast that she thought the opposition got the balance right but there was scope in the future to make changes.\n\"We made a very significant difference in these amendments and we think we've got the balance right. But of course, we've also announced that … one of the amendments will provide for a statutory review in three years,\" she says.\n\"So if the gambling companies think that they can get away with continuing improper conduct, if the broadcasters don't comply, if something is not working, the government — and hopefully it will be our government in three years' time — will be held to account.\"\nButler 'confident' Thriving Kids scheme to be rolled out on time by states\nThe real reason that Mark Butler is out and about this morning is of course the passage of the government's National Disability Insurance Scheme reforms.\nMore than 60 amendments were made last night in the Senate, with the bill expected to make its way back to the lower house for final approval later today.\nA key part of the reforms is the establishment of the Thriving Kids scheme. The program replaces support for children under eight with a development delay or with low to moderate autism who would no longer be supported by the NDIS under the changes.\nButler told ABC's News Breakfast he was \"very confident\" that every state bar Queensland would be able to roll out the scheme in time to avoid a gap in care.\n\"States recongise the importance of this. They've got their implementation plans ready for the 1st of October. Those services will start to roll out. They're not new concepts. These sort of services that we had in plce for many decades before the NDIS was created.\"\nKey Event\nSlashing tobacco excise a 'cheap headline': Butler\nOne Nation's proposal to slash the tobacco excise by 75 per cent has been slammed as a \"thought bubble\" and a \"cheap headline\" by Health Minister Mark Butler.\nButler conceded that nicotine on the black market was a \"serious law and order issue\" but said he wasn't convinced cutting the price of legal cigarettes was the answer.\n\"Governments aren't usually in the habit of engaging in price competition with organised crime. So, you know, that's a hard line for us to cross,\" he told ABC's News Breakfast.\n\"Equally, no-one's really said to me or the government where the price point [is] that actually starts to move them out of the market. I mean, you could get rid of the excise altogether, completely abolish it, and still legal cigarettes would be significantly more expensive than the illegal product.\n\"So that's why I think this requires more deep thought than frankly cheap headlines of the type we've seen this week.\"\nButler 'confident' in Treasury forecast on rental prices\nA note sent by NAB to its investors shows the bank is projecting a 30 per cent increase in rents in Sydney and Melbourne over the next two years, which it says is in response to the government's tax changes for property investors.\nBut Health Minister Mark Butler is standing by the Treasury's forecast the changes will only result in a $2 per week rental increase.\n\"We're very confident in our forecast. As Jim Chalmers has said, it's important to give these sorts of changes some time to settle down. Look over a couple of years, rather than a couple of months. But just think about it logically,\" he told ABC's News Breakfast.\nButler assured investors who owned a rental property on budget night and were already negatively geared that their arrangement could continue.\n\"I've seen some sort of pretty startling headlines, but it doesn't reflect logic and it doesn't reflect the modelling we're very confident about.\"\nUnions push for two-year rental lease minimum\nLandlords would have to offer renters at least two years on their lease, and the proportion of new public housing would increase fivefold as part of a union proposal being presented to the government.\nUnder current laws, the minimum for lease agreements varies across states and territories, and some, including New South Wales, have no rules in place at all.\nThe Australian Council of Trade Unions (ACTU) said a nationwide two-year minimum, with the option for renters to opt for a shorter period, would give renters more stability and allow them to plan.\n\"Having a standard of at least two years across the country would give renters the security of knowing they can stay in their home for at least that period of time,\" ACTU president Michele O'Neil said.\n\"This would deal with some of the fears that people currently have.\"\nThe union also wants a return to a Hawke-style model of construction, increasing the rate of public housing being built from one in 50 new homes to one in 10.\nKey Event\nNDIS overhaul passes Senate, to head back the lower house this morning\nThe federal government will pass its controversial National Disability Insurance Scheme reforms with the support of the Coalition, after they sailed through the Senate.\nLabor tabled 63 amendments aimed at cracking down on fraud and making the scheme more sustainable.\nThe legislation will return to the lower house this morning, when it will pass due to the government's majority in the chamber.\nGood morning 👋\nHello! Welcome to our politics live blog. I'm Courtney Gould from the ABC's Parliament House team, here to guide you through the day.\nIt was a day of deals yesterday as the government and Coalition came to an agreement on the NDIS overhaul, changes to gambling advertising, and a fix to the so-called widow's tax.\nIt was a big day! But what about today, I hear you ask. Well, it's an absolute smorgasbord of ministers and shadow ministers out and about this morning. Prime Minister Anthony Albanese will also welcome his Thai counterpart, Anutin Charnvirakul, for talks.\nIt'll probably be another busy day. So grab a coffee and let's dive in.\nLoading",
        "content": "live\nFederal politics live: Butler slams One Nation plan to slash tobacco excise as 'cheap headline'\nHealth Minister Mark Butler has slammed a One Nation plan to slash the tobacco excise, describing it as nothing but a \"cheap headline\".\nFollow all the updates in our live blog.\nSubmit a comment or question\nLive updates\nNew: Filters\nChoose what information you see below by using filters\n📸 Thai PM An...",
