        "title": "NT Health issues urgent call-out for dozens more hospital workers",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/nt-health-hospital-surge-workforce-calls-darwin-palmerston-staff/107050748",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417792+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/2a2517e0305724dbcc26ac0ca8e6b5a2?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=1078&cropW=1917&xPos=0&yPos=0&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Northern Territory Health's acting chief executive has issued a call-out for dozens of surge workers at the Darwin and Palmerston hospitals.\nIt comes as both hospitals have been under a code yellow — a period of critical patient demand and understaffing — since late July.\nWhat's next?\nThe nurses and midwives union says the NT government needs to consider better pay and conditions to alleviate long-term workforce shortages.\nThe head of the Northern Territory Health Department has issued an urgent call-out for almost 60 qualified health workers to relieve frontline staff under \"immense pressure\" at the Darwin and Palmerston hospitals.\nIn a sector-wide email sent on Tuesday, seen by the ABC, NT Health acting chief executive Susan Bowden called for expressions of interest from qualified nurses, midwives and pharmacists in the NT to take on up to two weeks of duties at Royal Darwin Hospital (RDH) and Palmerston Regional Hospital (PRH).\nIt comes as the two Top End hospitals have been in a code yellow — called when extraordinary levels of patient demand lead to emergency procedures being put in place — since July 31.\nIt is the sixth code yellow to be called at the hospitals since January.\nIn her email, Ms Bowden said high demand had led to the hospital's frontline staff being put under \"immense pressure\" and as a result, \"fatigue had set in\".\n\"Reducing the pressure on RDH and PRH and providing our staff with respite is my number one priority. As such, I'm calling on those who can help out to do so where possible,\" she said.\nShe said the department was seeking up to 57 qualified workers, including 20 emergency nurses, to take up redeployment to the hospital floor, from other areas of the health sector.\nIn a statement last week, an NT Health spokesperson said the department was investigating measures to relieve pressure, including a potential early opening of RDH's new 32-bed ward.\n\"We are throwing all of our resources and energy into this,\" Ms Bowden said in her email today.\nBetter conditions needed to fix long-term understaffing: union\nAustralian Nursing and Midwifery Federation (ANMF) NT branch secretary Heidi Crisp said frontline nurses were increasingly feeling the current code yellow \"is the worst one we've ever had\".\n\"They just want the public to know how desperate times are,\" she said.\nMs Crisp said the ANMF welcomed NT Health's call-out for surge workers, but warned it could lead to shortfalls in other departments.\n\"In ED [the emergency department] we've seen high-level nurses being on the floor I think most of the year,\" she said.\n\"People are telling me it's about 25 per cent of this year we've been in code yellow … so if those nurses are taken from their desks to do other work, it means all of that background work can't be done, and that has a big effect on the system.\"\nThe union representative said the ANMF's NT members had voted down the Territory government's latest offer in enterprise bargaining negotiations, but that improved pay and conditions would improve staffing levels at NT hospitals in the long-term.\n\"Right now, tempers are really high [and] members are outraged at the offer that they've been given,\" she said.\n\"[The NT government] need to have a really hard look at what we have put in our log of claims, and really consider those pay and conditions that we have requested.\"",
        "content": "Northern Territory Health's acting chief executive has issued a call-out for dozens of surge workers at the Darwin and Palmerston hospitals.\nIt comes as both hospitals have been under a code yellow — a period of critical patient demand and understaffing — since late July.\nWhat's next?\nThe nurses and midwives union says the NT government needs to consider better pay and conditions to alleviate long...",
