        "title": "Live: 'Coordinated mortgage fraud' uncovered by financial crimes watchdog",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/asx-markets-business-live-news/107051926",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417782+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/d5e4117e8ebb0040e593018db2d3003a?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2813&cropW=5000&xPos=0&yPos=260&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "live\nMarkets live updates: AUSTRAC uncovers 'coordinated mortgage fraud', ASX to fall for sixth day in a row\nAustralia's financial crimes regulator says it has uncovered \"coordinated mortgage fraud and systemic weaknesses\" across the nation's lending sector.\nThe Australian share market is likely to fall in morning trade, following another weak session on Wall Street.\nSee how the trading day unfolds on our blog.\nDisclaimer: this blog is not intended as investment advice.\nSubmit a comment or question\nLive updates\nNew: Filters\nChoose what information you see below by using filters\nKey Event\nAs pressure mounts for immigration cuts, big business warns about economic cost of 'emotive' debate\nThe boss of Australia's largest home builder Metricon, Brad Duggan, says there's been a 40% fall in inquiries to build new homes.\nHe also says a drop in the migration intake could further dent demand.\nBut he stresses the real issue is finding skilled workers to build more housing.\n\"I think it's important to realise that trades, these are real professions,\" he said.\n\"They're not something that you can really acquire and become honed in on overnight.\"\nMr Duggan has been meeting with Australia's housing minister on the issue is urging federal politicians to steer clear of emotive immigration debates and look at the facts.\nFor more, here's the story by my colleague Nassim Khadem:\nLoading...\nMarkets recap: Alan Kohler's finance report\nIf you need a refresher before the ASX opens for trading, I can certainly recommend Alan Kohler's finance report.\nAmong other things, Alan talked about CSL's sinking share price over the past couple of years and the price of copper hitting a record high, above $US14,000 a tonne — which provided a major boost to BHP's annual profit yesterday.\nLoading...\nKey Event\nCEO of Medical tech firm Pro Medicus dismisses software 'apocalypse'\nMedical imaging software company Pro Medicus has seen huge swings in its share price, partly due to the threat of AI and the so-called \"SaaSpocalypse\".\nFounder and CEO Sam Hupert says despite the share price being down 40 per cent over the past year, the company had its second-best year on record, and is experiencing the \"opposite\" of a software apocalypse.\nYou can listen to my interview with Dr Hupert here:\nLoading...\nKey Event\nWhat's behind CSL's $US2.6 billion annual loss?\nWhen I sat down to interview CSL's interim chief executive Gordon Naylor, I wasn't expecting him to be so refreshingly candid about his company's problems.\nThe biotech giant's $US2.6 billion full-year loss was driven by significant impairments on its balance sheet.\nParticularly at Vifor, a Swiss pharmaceutical that it bought four years ago for $US11.7 billion. Its value has been written down by several billions of dollars since then.\nWhen I asked whether it was a good investment, he said with a chuckle: \"Clearly not!\"\nCertainly not at that price tag.\nBut Mr Naylor, who has been the interim CEO since February, thinks the long term \"strategic rationale\" of acquiring an iron deficiency treatment division is sound.\nFor more, here's my full interview with him:\nLoading...\nKey Event\nQuest Apartment Hotels customer data exposed in security breach\nCustomers of Quest Apartment Hotels have been told their personal data has been exposed in a data security incident.\nIn an email to customers, seen by ABC News, Quest said the information that was part of the compromised data included full name, email address and other contact details.\n\"On Monday, 17 August 2026, we identified unauthorised access to a database system and immediately took steps to contain the incident,\" the email read.\n\"The incident arose from a vulnerability through our third party service provider. The incident has been contained.\"\nThe company said it had reported the incident to authorities including the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner.\n\"We are very sorry this has happened and for any concern it may cause. Protecting the privacy and security of our customers is extremely important to us,\" David Mansfield, managing director for Australasia at The Ascott Limited, said in the email.\n\"We will contact you if our investigation identifies any further information that is relevant to you or if there are any additional steps you need to take.\"\nThe Ascott Limited is the company behind Quest and other accommodation brands including Citadines and Oakwood, operating in Australia and globally.\nWe have contacted Quest and The Ascott Limited for more information, including the scale of the breach.\nAre you affected or know more? Let us know in the comments, and leave your contact details so we can get in touch.\nKey Event\nWhitehaven Coal full-year profit drops 41 per cent on lower coal prices\nIt's another big day of corporate results and Whitehaven Coal is one of the companies that have just released their numbers.\nThe coal miner reported its statutory full-year profit fell 41% to $385 million.\nLast year, Whitehaven sold 30% of its stake in the Blackwater coking coal mine in Queensland to Japan's Nippon Steel and JFE Steel.\nThat \"sell down\" earned the miner $391 million, which was recognised in the previous year's annual results.\nSo this year, its profit fell sharply because the previous result was boosted by earnings from a major transaction.\nWhen one-off \"significant items\" like that are excluded, the company's underlying annual profit was still weaker — down 29% to $227 million.\nThe company attributes that falling profit to lower realised coal prices or \"cyclical price weakness\".\nThat fell below analysts' underlying profit estimates of $247.6 million, according to Visible Alpha data.\n- with Reuters\nKey Event\nAustralian tech stocks may follow Wall Street giants lower\nThe Australian technology sector is tiny and only accounts for about 3% of the share market.\nHowever, there's a chance local tech stocks may not perform so well when the ASX opens for trading.\nParticularly if the negative sentiment on Wall Street overnight spreads here.\nThe S&P 500 lost 0.7%, to 7,692 points, while the Nasdaq Composite shed 1.3% to 26,290 points. Both stock indexes posted their worst trading days since July 29.\nThe Dow Jones Industrial Average fell by a modest 0.2%, to 53,343 points.\nWhich tech companies were hit hardest?\nSome of the biggest falls were recorded by semiconductor and microchip stocks including Sandisk, Marvell, Western Digital, Micron and Intel, which dropped between 7% and 9% each.\nSharp falls were also observed across AI-linked companies, including Meta (-4.5%), AMD (-4.3%), Broadcom (-3.2%), Oracle (-2.6%), Nvidia (-2.3%), Dell -(2.3%) and SpaceX (-2%).\nThe surge in government borrowing costs — as evidenced by US, Japan and Germany bond yields rising to multi-decade highs — is bad news for the more speculative parts of the share market.\nHigher long-term interest rates tend to affect the share price of tech companies particularly hard.\nThat's due to the lofty valuations of tech companies — when comparing their share price with earnings — and the high levels of debt on their books.\nMuch of this debt, which is becoming more expensive, has been used to fund the rapid building of AI data centres across the world.\nKey Event\nUnions push for two-year rental lease minimum and massive public housing build\nLandlords would have to offer renters at least two years on their lease, and the proportion of new public housing would increase fivefold as part of a union proposal being presented to the government.\nUnder current laws, the minimum for lease agreements varies across states and territories, and some, including New South Wales, have no rules in place at all.\nThe Australian Council of Trade Unions (ACTU) said a nationwide two-year minimum, with the option for renters to opt for a shorter period, would give renters more stability and allow them to plan.\n\"Having a standard of at least two years across the country would give renters the security of knowing they can stay in their home for at least that period of time,\" ACTU president Michele O'Neil said.\n\"This would deal with some of the fears that people currently have.\"\nFor more, here's the story by Holly Tregenza:\nKey Event\nNo evidence of 'widespread' money laundering found in mortgage fraud investigation, AUSTRAC says\nSpeaking about AUSTRAC's findings of \"coordinated mortgage fraud\" that were mostly linked with Sydney properties, the regulator's chief executive Brendan Thomas said the vulnerabilities could not be addressed by institutions acting alone.\n“The scale of this activity should be a wake-up call for every lender,\" Mr Thomas said.\n\"The same warning signs were found across banks that together cover the vast majority of Australia's mortgage market.\n“While this project did not identify evidence of widespread money laundering, the weaknesses it exposed could be exploited by criminals seeking to abuse Australia's financial system.”\nAUSTRAC said it had provided the names of individuals and entities potentially involved in submission of false documents in support of loan applications various agencies, including Australian Securities and Investments Commission (ASIC), the Australian Taxation Office (ATO) and Tax Practitioners Board.\n\"Participating banks have used intelligence generated through Operation Claw to identify potentially fraudulent loans, investigate suspicious activity, strengthen controls and make further referrals to the appropriate authorities,\" the regulator added.\n\"Some banking relationships have been ended, and further action is expected.\n\"AUSTRAC has issued a call to arms to every mortgage lender in Australia, urging them to examine their mortgage books for signs of fraud, report suspicious activity and implement strong controls to shut this behaviour out of the Australian economy.\"\nThe financial crimes regulator said it worked closely with Australian banks, the ATO, NSW Police Force, NSW Crime Commission, Australian Criminal Intelligence Commission, APRA and ASIC throughout its investigation.\nJapanese bonds mostly held by Japanese investors\nAs I know absolutely nothing about the bond market , could someone please explain why an investor would buy Japanese bonds at 3% return , when they can get 5% buying US or Australian bonds. I thought the Japanese economy was a bit more volatile.and risky ?\n- Phillip\nHi Phillip, I am no bond expert either, but I do know that the overwhelming bulk of Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs) are held by Japanese investors.\nAccording to this document I found from Japan's Ministry of Finance, last year about 87% of JGBs were held by domestic investors, so only 13% by offshore investors.\nGiven the transaction and currency hedging costs/risks of Japanese investors buying offshore bonds, it makes sense that JGBs can offer a lower return and still attract domestic investment.\nAlso, the Bank of Japan alone holds close to 50% of the government's debt, and it isn't buying or holding for the investment returns!\nKey Event\n'Coordinated mortgage fraud' and 'fraudulent loans' exposed by AUSTRAC\nAustralia's financial crimes regulator says it has uncovered \"coordinated mortgage fraud and systemic weaknesses\" across the nation's lending sector.\nAnalysis of data from 10 major banks has identified potentially hundreds of millions of dollars worth of \"fraudulent loans\", mostly linked to properties in Sydney, according to the Australian Transaction Reports and Analysis Centre (AUSTRAC).\nThe regulator called its investigation Operation Claw, which found the suspected fraud involved:\n- inflated incomes,\n- misrepresented employment, and\n- fabricated or unverifiable business activity.\nIt also identified cases where offshore or third-party funds were used to complete property settlements and make mortgage repayments.\nAUSTRAC said this conduct demonstrated \"how false income streams and complex funding arrangements can facilitate access to the Australian property market\".\nIt also said this fraudulent activity was not confined to just one lender or borrower group.\n\"Recurring warning signs across participating banks included falsified or misleading documents and the repeated use of mortgage brokers, accountants and law firms across multiple loan applications,\" AUSTRAC said in a statement.\nWe'll have more details on this story in a minute!\nKey Event\nUnlicensed telemarketers targeted in government crackdown on super switching schemes\nUnlicensed telemarketers who cold call and make unsolicited approaches to convince people to switch their super will be banned under long-awaited reforms.\nAssistant Treasurer Daniel Mulino will appear at the National Press Club today and announce a suite of changes to better protect Australians from operators that rob them of their retirement savings.\nThe reforms follow the collapse of the Shield and First Guardian funds, which saw 12,000 people lose more than $1 billion in retirement savings and exposed deep flaws in the regulation of Australia's almost $4.5 trillion retirement savings pool.\nCurrently, people might go on Facebook and click an ad that looks innocent, like \"Find Your Lost Super\" or \"Check Your Super\", but then gives permission for a telemarketer, also known as a \"lead generator\", to contact them and suggest they connect with a financial adviser to switch their super.\nMr Mulino said that, under the changes, lead generators would need to be licensed to advertise.\nFor more, here's the story by Nassim Khadem:\nKey Event\nAustralian government's borrowing costs at their highest level in years\nConcerns about the US-Iran war dragging on, expensive oil prices, high inflation and ballooning government debt are clearly on the minds of Australian investors.\nThe Australian government's 10-year bond yield is sitting at 5.07%, around its highest level in many years.\nLike many overseas governments, its long-term borrowing costs have also surged as lenders demand more compensation for what they see as higher risks over the next decade.\nKey Event\nUS, Japan and Germany bond yields surge to multi-decade highs on 'problematic' government spending\nShare markets in the US and Europe fell overnight due, in part, to concerns about elevated oil prices and persistently high inflation across the world.\nThat, in turn, led to several governments' long-term borrowing costs rising to their highest levels in decades:\n- United States: 30-year Treasury yield rose above 5%, its highest level since 2007, during the global financial crisis.\n- Japan: 10-year bond yield climbed to its highest in three decades, at just under 3%.\n- Germany: 30-year bond yield hit its highest since 2011.\n- France: 30-year bond yield is now at its highest point since 2008.\nThere is growing concern about ballooning government debt and geopolitics.\nWhen it costs more for governments to borrow money through the bond market, that also leads to higher interest repayments for businesses and households.\nAnd generally, when it becomes more expensive to borrow, the investors' tolerance for risk falls — along with risky investments, like shares.\n'Problematic' spending by politicians\nBond markets are entering an era where the inflation and interest rate outlook is more uncertain as US President Donald Trump's policies — from tariffs to war — upend the global order.\nDebt levels in developed countries are reaching thresholds that look increasingly unsustainable, with the US debt pile nearing $US40 trillion.\nThe war in Iran is dragging on, pushing oil prices above $US90 per barrel — pushing inflation higher and hurting global economic growth.\nIn addition, massive borrowing by technology companies to fund a buildout of artificial intelligence infrastructure is competing with demand for government bonds.\nBond yields' recent surge \"suggests investors are losing patience with fiscal profligacy,\" said Jonas Goltermann, chief markets economist at Capital Economics.\nBut he noted that it's \"entirely unsurprising: the fiscal outlook in several major economies is problematic, and politicians have shown little appetite for addressing the issue\".\n- with Reuters\nKey Event\nASX to fall for sixth straight day, oil price hits three-week high\nGood morning, and welcome to the ABC's finance blog! I'll be guiding you through the latest market action for the next few hours.\nThe local share market is on track to fall for its sixth trading day in a row.\nASX futures, which are a rough indicator of how the market may open, have slipped by about 0.2%.\nThe Australian dollar is also a little weaker at 70.8 US cents.\nIt follows another downbeat session on Wall Street with its benchmark index, the S&P 500, dropping for its third straight day.\nOil prices have risen to their highest level in more than three weeks on fading hopes of a resolution to the US-Iran war any time soon.\nThat's after Iran said it would adopt a more offensive stance and the Strait of Hormuz would remain closed, while the United States has ruled out extending their ceasefire.\nBrent crude futures rose 0.5% to $US91.35 per barrel.\nUS West Texas Intermediate crude futures jumped 1% to $US85.33 a barrel.\nBoth oil contracts are trading at their most expensive level since July 24.\nAnyway, please grab a coffee, tea or whatever you normally have in the morning, and I'll have more updates for you shortly!\nMarket snapshot\n- ASX futures: -0.2% to 8,893 points\n- ASX 200 (Tuesday close): -0.04% at 9,070 points\n- Australian dollar: -0.3% to 70.8 US cents\n- Wall Street: Dow Jones: (-0.2%), S&P 500 (-0,7%) , Nasdaq Composite (-1.3%)\n- Europe: FTSE (+0.1%), DAX (-0.8%), Stoxx 600 (-0.7%)\n- Spot gold: -1.8% to $US4,338/ounce\n- Oil (Brent futures): +0.2% to $US91.07/barrel\n- Iron ore: +0.2% to $US95.75/tonne\n- Bitcoin: +0.4% to $US64,617\nPrices current around 7:30am AEST",
