        "title": "Accidental fire destroys family home in South West WA",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/boyup-brook-house-fire-destroyed-family-home/107050394",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417778+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/27700cea797742ff22dfbd896cd24277?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=1701&cropW=3024&xPos=0&yPos=1030&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Boyup Brook mother's escape from horror fire that destroyed family home\nIn short:\nA Western Australian mother is grappling with the reality of a house fire that destroyed her home and belongings.\nLisa Fawcett was asleep at her home in southern WA when the blaze began.\nWhat's next?\nThe Department of Fire and Emergency Services says it has not yet determined a cause of the non-suspicious fire due to the extent of damage.\nA family is lucky to be alive after a fire decimated their home in Western Australia's South West and destroyed everything they own.\nLisa Fawcett was asleep at the Boyup Brook house, about 260 kilometres south of Perth, on Sunday morning when a fire ignited.\nWhile an official cause of the fire has not been determined by the Department of Fire and Emergency Services (DFES), Ms Fawcett said she believed a bar heater was left too close to a couch.\n\"I had everything I needed, and now I have nothing,\" she said.\n\"It's hard to comprehend.\"\n'I just had to get out'\nMs Fawcett said she was woken by her son, whose room was the first to be filled with thick black smoke.\nStill in her pyjamas, she rushed to grab her car keys.\n\"All I could see was red flames,\" she said.\n\"By the time I was able to walk across the lounge room, the whole ceiling was just this really thick, choking black smoke.\"\nAs she walked towards the verandah to get the keys, she said the glass exploded around her.\n\"That's when I knew I didn't have time to grab anything, I just had to get out,\"\nMeanwhile, Ms Fawcett's son went to collect belongings from his room.\n\"It was too late, and he had to jump out the window to escape the fire,\" she said.\nEmergency services were called to the property just after 10am on Sunday.\nMs Fawcett, who has narcolepsy, said she was in shock.\n\"It was just so quick. It was like three or four minutes to get to the reality of what was actually happening,\" she said.\n\"I have trouble waking up … that made it harder for me to even think what to do.\"\nIrreplaceable loss\nMs Fawcett said her house was underinsured, but a payout would likely be enough to clear the site of debris.\nBut she said the sentimental possessions lost to the fire hurt the most.\n\"I lost my partner seven years ago, so all the engagement rings and personal things and photos of him and that, I don't have anything,\" she said.\n\"Fifty years of collections and just all the antiques and grandma's jewellery and stuff like that is all gone.\"\nDuring the fire, neighbours helped disconnect gas bottles and move her car.\nSince the incident, Ms Fawcett said she had been offered temporary accommodation in Bridgetown.\n\"The community has been really supportive,\" she said.\nSouth West Acting Superintendent John Bowman said the fire was seemingly caused by a heater.\n\"As far as we know at this stage, it was caused by an electrical fault with a heater, or the heater was too close to some fabric [and] caused the house to go up,\" he said.\nActing Superintendent Bowman said it was a relief the family managed to escape relatively unscathed.\n\"The injuries were more smoke-related, not [major] burns as such, is my understanding, so that's a good result,\" he said.",
