    {
        "title": "Lawyers replace jockeys in 'chaos' of camel race",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/white-cliffs-camel-cup-outback-event-far-west-nsw/107049888",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417761+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/7ca34d7a5224cdb449a865217f7745a2?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2525&cropW=4489&xPos=511&yPos=803&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Lawyers saddle up for White Cliffs' annual Camel Cup\nIn short:\nMore than 600 people attended the annual White Cliffs Camel Cup and Bikekhana on Saturday.\nThe unique race sees punters from the crowd volunteer to race camels in six different heats.\nIn one such race, four lawyers and a builder threw their hats into the ring.\nIn the remote opal mining town of White Cliffs, four lawyers and a builder prepare to do something few professionals would put on their resume: camel racing.\nMost of them have never ridden a camel before, much less raced one.\nAs they strap on their helmets, they listen intently as a woman in denim flares and a cowboy hat explains how best to avoid falling off.\n\"When they're standing up, just hold on and lean back slightly,\" she says.\n\"And [above] everything else, just hang on for dear life.\"\nNo experience, no worries\nThe annual Camel Cup is one of the outback's more unusual traditions, involving locals rather than professional jockeys.\nCup day involves six heats, all of them featuring volunteer jockeys.\nIn this particular race, five Broken Hill locals with no camel riding experience have varying levels of confidence.\nHarry Fisher, a lawyer from Wollongong who moved to Broken Hill earlier this year, is one of the more self-assured racers and has his heart set on winning, except there is one small problem: he has never ridden a camel before.\n\"I'd heard a rumour a few weeks ago that people who come along get a chance [to race on a camel],\" he says.\n\"As soon as I heard that, I thought, 'I just want to get involved in this camel racing.' I'm doing this for the story.\"\nCompeting against him is his friend and fellow lawyer Danyon O'Rourke, who has set the bar considerably lower.\n\"My main goal really is to stay on the camel,\" he says.\nAs the five racers climb aboard, it becomes clear that staying on might be easier said than done for Danyon.\nHis camel seems less than enthusiastic about the arrangement, repeatedly deciding, without warning, to sit down.\nFelicity Forge, another lawyer who also only recently moved to Broken Hill, admits she came into the race without much of a strategy.\n\"I had absolutely no plan,\" she laughs.\n\"Just stay on, stand up.\"\nFelicity had not planned on entering the camel race at all when she arrived at the Camel Cup.\n\"It was peer pressure, mostly,\" she says.\nHow to pick a winning ride\nThe race is just as much a gamble for spectators as it is for riders.\nAuctioneer David Whittenbury encourages punters to place their bets on which camel will win — though he admits there is no secret formula for picking the winner.\n\"It's chaos, that's the only way to describe it,\" he says.\n\"It's all about the camel's desire.\"\nAt least some of the riders attempt to devise a game plan.\nOliver Sinclair, also a lawyer, stretched in preparation while discussing his strategy with fellow rider Adam Collier, the only person in the heat who is not a lawyer.\n\"I'm going to serenade my camel … it's going to carry me home,\" he tells Adam.\nFor Danyon, however, the strategy is less about winning and more about getting his reluctant camel to the starting line.\nMultiple attempts by camel handlers to coax his beast into moving prove unsuccessful as it repeatedly lies down in the middle of the track.\n\"[As] we walked to the starting line, the trainers were talking between each other and said they would take my camel back to the stables after my race,\" he says, presumably due to the animal's reluctance to participate.\nThese comments do not ease his doubts about his chances.\nAnd they're racing\nEventually, the camels are ready to race.\nThe woman in flare jeans takes her cowboy hat off to signal the start, and spectators scale the fence to cheer the riders on as the camels charge past.\nImmediately, Felicity and her camel cut away from the pack, taking a clear lead, while Danyon's camel seems to be facing in the wrong direction.\nAmid a cloud of dust, the camels lumber around the track, looking more awkward than athletic.\nThe race lasts about 30 seconds, with Felicity and her camel taking out the top spot.\nAfter the race, Harry concedes he is disappointed with placing third.\n\"It was a hard-fought race,\" he says.\n\"I didn't win, but I didn't come last, that's the main thing.\"\nDanyon, who did come last, may have been the real winner.\nHe achieved the only goal he had set himself: he stayed on.\nMore volunteer jockeys than ever before\nThe White Cliffs Camel Cup and Bikekhana has been running about five years, according to club secretary Honor Taylor, though previous events have not seen quite as many punters keen to climb aboard.\n\"In previous years, if you want to come and ride, you just sign a waiver and put your name down,\" she says.\n\"Normally, people get to have two or three races. But this year I just had too many jockeys — so many people wanted to come and have a go.\"\nAfter a slow start to tourist season across the region, Honor says the numbers who turned out for the race will have a huge impact on the community.\n\"We probably had about 650 through the gate,\" she says.\n\"It's a good boost to the town.\n\"We're probably about 90 per cent down on tourism, basically because of fuel prices and the cost of living, so these events boost up the town and businesses and accommodation.\"\nInitially, the plan was to host the White Cliffs Bikekhana every year, with the camel races an addition to the event every second year.\nBut the races became so popular, they became an annual fixture.\n\"We did camels and bikes and were going to do camels every second year, so the next year we just did bikes, and it wasn't as big or good,\" Honor says.\n\"So yes, it'll be an annual event … A lot of people have never seen them before … everyone loves it.\"",
        "content": "Lawyers saddle up for White Cliffs' annual Camel Cup\nIn short:\nMore than 600 people attended the annual White Cliffs Camel Cup and Bikekhana on Saturday.\nThe unique race sees punters from the crowd volunteer to race camels in six different heats.\nIn one such race, four lawyers and a builder threw their hats into the ring.\nIn the remote opal mining town of White Cliffs, four lawyers and a builder p...",
