    {
        "title": "Closed highway gets $100m overtaking lane after community push",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/federal-government-great-western-highway-funding/107052766",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417758+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/17ab1bf03089f77f82bf61949d4549f6?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2812&cropW=4999&xPos=0&yPos=0&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Great Western Highway gets $100 million federal funding for overtaking lane\nIn short:\nThe Commonwealth has announced $100 million in funding for the construction of an additional lane along the Great Western Highway.\nIt matches the NSW government's commitment to repair the partially closed road.\nWhat's next?\nThe highway is set to reopen in the second quarter of 2027.\nCommunity pressure has paid off after the federal government committed $100 million to building an additional lane on the main road connecting Sydney to Western New South Wales.\nThe Great Western Highway has been shut at Mount Victoria since March, after cracking was discovered in the 200-year-old Mitchell's Causeway.\nThe Commonwealth funding has matched the NSW government's commitment, allowing the construction of an additional eastbound climbing lane.\nCommunity members and local politicians have been calling for the third lane during the construction, which will allow for the overtaking of slow-moving vehicles and trucks\nThe construction of the additional lane is expected to begin towards the end of the restoration works.\nNew South Wales Minister for Roads Jenny Aitchison said it would not change the timeline for reopening the road.\n\"The timeline for the project is the same as what we had established,\"\n\"We are working as hard as we can to try and bring that forward but [2027] is the estimated completion date.\"\nAdditional lane\nThe local Member for Calare, Andrew Gee, raised the lack of funding for a third lane on social media in June.\n\"They're only installing one lane each way over the Convict Bridge, so we're not going to get any better access,\" Mr Gee said.\n\"They're talking about installing a third lane at some stage in the future, but there's no date for that and there's certainly no funding for it.\"\nOriginal design plans released earlier this year included the provision for a third lane. However, Ms Aitchinson said at the time that the project would open as single-lane access first.\nShe said the lane would ensure efficiency for drivers.\n\"[It will] enable heavy freight to have a climbing range up through the pass … often it's very difficult for them to get up the hill and so this will really make that a much easier process,\" Ms Aitchinson said.\n\"The extra climbing lane will ensure that when heavy vehicles are on the pass, that the light vehicles will be able to pass them.\"\nConstruction underway\nIn June, the state government employed a contractor to construct a new road deck on the existing causeway supported by piles anchored to the bedrock below, with the highway expected to reopen in the second quarter of 2027.\nMore than 150 workers have been inducted onto the project.\nThe closure of the main route over the Blue Mountains between Sydney and Western New South Wales has led to long diversions and delays for trucks and commuters, while businesses have experienced major downturns.\n\"The Michell's Causeway was the key link between the mountains and the Central West,\" federal Transport Minister Catherine King said.\n\"The cracking has cut off communities and made travelling through the region challenging.\n\"This will not be a simple repair, but getting this done is a priority.\"",
        "content": "Great Western Highway gets $100 million federal funding for overtaking lane\nIn short:\nThe Commonwealth has announced $100 million in funding for the construction of an additional lane along the Great Western Highway.\nIt matches the NSW government's commitment to repair the partially closed road.\nWhat's next?\nThe highway is set to reopen in the second quarter of 2027.\nCommunity pressure has paid of...",
