        "full_content": "live\nLive: Mushroom killer Erin Patterson appeals murder conviction\nConvicted murderer Erin Patterson is today seeking to have her conviction overturned, while Crown prosecutors will seek to have her sentence increased and the prospect of parole removed.\nFollow our coverage live.\nLive updates\nNew: Filters\nChoose what information you see below by using filters\nWhat can we expect today?\nIt is expected the appeals will take place over two days of hearings, with lawyers for Patterson and crown prosecutors both laying out their cases for appeals.\nPatterson has engaged barristers Richard Edney and Veronika Drago for her appeal, who will argue there were multiple miscarriages of justice and prejudicial incidents during her trial.\nDirector of Public Prosecutions Brendan Kissane will lead the prosecution and will argue the sentence given to Patterson, which allowed for the possibility of parole one day, was inadequate and should be strengthened.\nPatterson is not expected to attend in person, and will instead watch the proceedings via video link.\nThe view from outside Courtroom One\nUnlike the murder trial itself, which was held in the Victorian town of Morwell, today's hearing will instead be held at the Supreme Court in Melbourne's CBD.\nWhile a hearing like this would usually be heard at the Court of Appeal, the decision was made to move it to the larger court next door, given the immense public interest in the case.\nAbout 40 people, mostly reporters, are crammed into a hallway in the Supreme Court waiting to enter Courtroom One.\nA short time ago, mushroom poisoning victim Ian Wilkinson and other family members were escorted into the courtroom.\nLegal teams for the prosecution and defence followed. Lead police investigator Stephen Eppingstall, along with other homicide detectives from Victoria Police, is also here.\nHow did we get here?\nThe Erin Patterson murder trial was one of the highest-profile cases in recent memory, dominating global headlines for months.\nFor a recap on the deadly mushroom lunch, the media frenzy surrounding the case, and the murder trial watched by millions, catch up with this wonderful feature story from the ABC's Kristian Silva and Joseph Dunstan:\nKey Event\nHello and welcome to our coverage\nErin Patterson's legal team will head to the Court of Appeal today, in a bid to have her murder conviction overturned.\nPatterson was convicted in July last year and sentenced to life in jail, with a non-parole period of 33 years.\nMeanwhile, Crown prosecutors will launch a separate appeal to remove any possibility of parole.\nWe'll bring you all the events of the day, as they unfold during the hearing, which is being held at the Supreme Court.",
