    {
        "title": "Bush kids raise thousands for charities with 19yo bike ride fundraiser",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/outback-kids-continue-bike-ride-fundraiser/107046802",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-18T23:58:47.417747+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/1f36ce8aebe2fb512b73be4647afc7da?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=3375&cropW=6000&xPos=0&yPos=625&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "feel good\nOutback kids remember community members with annual bike ride fundraiser\nOnce a year, traffic along the Flinders Highway at Prairie comes to a standstill as dozens of brightly coloured pushbike riders cross the road, their wheels kicking up the red dust.\nDespite being a town of only 100 people, Prairie, about 340 kilometres south-west of Townsville, pulls out all stops for its annual bike-ride fundraiser.\nFor 19 years, the Tour De Prairie, run by the school, has raised thousands of dollars for charities important to members of the community.\nSchool P&C treasurer Bernie Merton said about $1,000 was raised each year.\n\"I think it's really good, everyone enjoys it, and older boarding school kids come back for it and it's just nice to jump on a bike and go for a ride,\" she said.\nThis year, donations went to the Royal Flying Doctor Service (RFDS), with the event being held in memory of local boy Conall Wescott, who died in May from the rare genetic condition Gould syndrome.\nConall's father, Hugh Westcott, said the RFDS was vital in supporting his family.\n\"We had multiple flights to Townsville where they kept him alive, they saved his life numerous times,\"\nIn memory of locals\nEach year, students from Prairie State School choose a charity to raise money for.\nIn previous years, they have supported Cows for Cambodia, Cancer Council, and have raised money for the chemotherapy treatment room at the Hughenden hospital.\nParents, teachers, students and the wider community ride their pushbikes 7km out to Railview cattle station, where they play games and eat lunch.\nRailview grazier Michelle Fryer has supported the cause since it started in 2007, when it raised money for the Cerebral Palsy Alliance, in memory of her daughter Alex.\n\"She's always in everyone's memory, there's memories of her at the school still,\" she said.\nAlex died just days after her eighth birthday from complications of cerebral palsy.\n\"That was the first year and then, after that, we just said, 'It doesn't always have to be about Alex, it just needs to be somebody in need,'\" Ms Fryer said.\n\"Prairie's a great community and it's just about giving back.\"\nMs Fryer reflects on some memorable years of the fundraiser.\n\"Those first few years, there were a lot of flat tyres and the parents all started getting savvier.\"\nShe said she hoped to see it continue for years to come.\n\"We do it for the kids, and we're happy to keep doing it until the children don't want to.\"\nCaravanners supporting the cause\nMs Merton said the children received a lot of donations from tourists or people passing through town.\n\"It gives them confidence when they go around the caravan parks and ask the tourists and strike up a conversation,\" she said.\n\"It's been quiet this year, but the ones that have come through are very supportive.\"",
        "content": "feel good\nOutback kids remember community members with annual bike ride fundraiser\nOnce a year, traffic along the Flinders Highway at Prairie comes to a standstill as dozens of brightly coloured pushbike riders cross the road, their wheels kicking up the red dust.\nDespite being a town of only 100 people, Prairie, about 340 kilometres south-west of Townsville, pulls out all stops for its annual bik...",
