        "title": "Man convicted in woman's 2017 trailer hitch death in Thunder Bay, Ont., granted full parole",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/thunder-bay/brayden-bushby-granted-full-parole-9.7311268?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.649590+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1.4202208,1787077310514/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C304%2C3264%2C1836%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Man convicted in woman's 2017 trailer hitch death in Thunder Bay, Ont., granted full parole\nBrayden Bushby sentenced in 2021 for manslaughter of Barbara Kentner of Wabigoon Lake Ojibway Nation\nWARNING: This story contains details of violence against Indigenous women, as well as references to racist beliefs and behaviours. Resources can be found at the bottom of this story.\nThe man convicted of manslaughter in the 2017 death of Barbara Kentner in Thunder Bay, Ont., has been granted full parole with conditions.\nThe decision regarding Brayden Bushby, who was sentenced five years ago to eight years in prison, was made by the Parole Board of Canada (PBC) on Thursday, according to documents provided to CBC News on Tuesday.\n“The board considered all relevant available information including file information, victim information, Correctional Service of Canada's recommendation and the oral presentation at the panel hearing,\" the documents say.\nIt said releasing Bushby on full parole won't pose \"undue risk\" and \"will contribute to the protection of society\" by facilitating his reintegration \"back into society as a law-abiding citizen.\"\nKentner, 34, died from medical complications in the months after being struck by a trailer hitch thrown at her from a moving vehicle. She was a member of Wabigoon Lake Ojibway Nation, and is remembered by her family as a loving mother, sister, cousin and aunt.\nBushby was sentenced in June 2021. Due to time already served, the prison term was reduced to seven years and 11 months. The now 27-year-old has two children.\nCBC News has reached out to Kentner’s family and Wabigoon Lake Ojibway Nation for comment.\nBushby was first granted day parole in August 2023. It was revoked in July 2024 after the PBC said he violated various conditions. At that time, Kentner's sister, Melissa Kentner, told CBC News, “I thought I was going to have a heart attack” upon hearing the news.\nHe was granted day parole again in December 2025 for six months and denied full parole, and granted three more months of day parole in June 2026 in a community-based residential facility, which the PBC says is also a substance abuse treatment centre.\nFull parole allows an offender “to serve part of their sentence under supervision in the community under specific conditions,” according to the government of Canada's website. “Offenders on full parole typically reside in a private residence.”\nNAN calls parole decision 'devastating news'\nNishnawbe Aski Nation (NAN), a political-territorial organization that represents 49 First Nations across Treaties 9 and 5, issued a statement Tuesday afternoon in response to Bushby's release, calling it “devastating news for Barbara Kentner’s family, the community of Wabigoon Lake Ojibway First Nation and the entire Thunder Bay community.”\n“We are extremely disappointed to learn that her attacker has been granted full parole and that parole officials have deemed him suitable for reintegration into society. This feels like a profound injustice,\" NAN said.\nDuring Bushby’s pre-release hearing on Thursday, he attributed his offence “to lots of drinking and bad decisions.”\n“You denied targeting the victim [Kentner] and maintained what happened was a result of having been drinking for three days and making stupid decisions,” the PBC documents say.\nAfter Bushby was first granted day parole, he got a Confederate flag tattooed on his chest.\n“You said you once purchased a truck from the United States with a Confederate flag and liked it. You have a southern heritage and the choice of your tattoo was not about racism,” the PBC documents say.\n“You agreed the tattoo was one of your choices, and say you fell asleep during the process and were unaware of the final tattoo until it was completed.”\n- Man convicted in 2017 trailer hitch death in Thunder Bay gets day parole extension\n- Brayden Bushby gets day parole in Barbara Kentner trailer hitch death in Thunder Bay after 1st one was revoked\nBushby was also involved in a minor vehicle collision in March 2024, left the scene and failed to report what happened to his case management team.\n“It was also noted that you were suspected to be involved in the theft of a motor vehicle,” say earlier documents from the PBC.\nThe following month, Bushby was pulled over and found to be under the influence of alcohol.\n- Day parole revoked for Brayden Bushby, man convicted of trailer hitch death of Barbara Kentner in Thunder Bay\n- Barbara Kentner has been failed again, says family after Brayden Bushby gets day parole in trailer-hitch death\nIn August 2025, the parole board said he was referred to work with a Black offender liaison officer after being observed on video surveillance “cutting a swastika into the institutional lawn.”\nIn discussing this incident, “you said it was not your finest moment and that you were not thinking clearly. You did not fully comprehend or understand what it symbolizes, the connection to the atrocities until you took the programming with the Black Cultural Advisor,” the PBC documents say.\nNAN's statement said “the parole board’s own decision acknowledges that Bushby has shown a repeated pattern of high-risk behaviour.”\n“We reject the board’s conclusion that releasing Bushby on full parole will not pose undue risk to society. Anyone with even the most basic understanding of the racism and violence being perpetrated on Indigenous people in Thunder Bay should be able to determine that he remains a risk.\n\"Granting him parole sends a dangerous and misguided message.”\n'This is what systemic failure looks like'\nThe PBC acknowledged the many victim impact statements from Kentner’s family and friends as well as Indigenous organizations and representations. They speak “to the impacts of another Indigenous woman losing her life, and the huge impacts on the community given the intergenerational impacts of historical and recent losses and trauma,” it said.\nBushby must follow seven conditions to maintain full parole, including:\n- Following a treatment plan/program related to substance abuse, attitude, mental health and emotions management.\n- Not associating with individuals known or suspected to be involved in criminal activity and substance abuse.\n- Not consuming, purchasing or possessing drugs besides prescribed medication.\n- Not consuming alcohol.\n- Not entering establishments that primarily sell alcohol.\n- Not contacting Kentner’s family, directly or indirectly.\n- Reporting all sexual and non-sexual relationships and friendships to his parole supervisor.\nCora McGuire-Cyrette is chief executive officer of the Ontario Native Women's Association (ONWA).\nIn an interview with CBC News on Tuesday about Bushby's release, she said “this is what systemic failure looks like.”\n“Homicide against us as Indigenous women has doubled since the national inquiry. And so we as a society, we as a community, we need to question what has changed since [Kentner's] death.\n“Where is the investments into prevention of future death? Are we going to be doing anything more than bringing awareness to this issue? Awareness without action will not equal any change, and this is what we're seeing here.”\nSupport is available for anyone affected by the issue of missing and murdered Indigenous people. Immediate emotional assistance and crisis support is available 24/7 through a national hotline at 1-844-413-6649.\nMental health counselling and crisis support is also available 24 hours a day, seven days a week through the Hope for Wellness hotline at 1-855-242-3310 or by online chat at www.hopeforwellness.ca.",
        "content": "Man convicted in woman's 2017 trailer hitch death in Thunder Bay, Ont., granted full parole\nBrayden Bushby sentenced in 2021 for manslaughter of Barbara Kentner of Wabigoon Lake Ojibway Nation\nWARNING: This story contains details of violence against Indigenous women, as well as references to racist beliefs and behaviours. Resources can be found at the bottom of this story.\nThe man convicted of man...",
