        "title": "Prime Minister Philip Davis’s National Address: A New Energy Era Update",
        "link": "https://www.bahamaspress.com/prime-minister-philip-daviss-national-address-a-new-energy-era-update/",
        "source": "Bahamaspress.com",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.649438+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.bahamaspress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/maxresdefault.jpg",
        "full_content": "My fellow Bahamians, good evening.\nIn October 2022, when I first spoke to you in detail about BPL, I did not leave the harsh reality of the state of our electricity infrastructure and the hundreds of millions needed to fix our aging systems to someone else.\nI came before you myself, because the buck stops with me.\nEver since, I have been honest about the scale of this challenge.\nIn April of 2025, I stood in the House of Assembly and gave this country the most complete accounting of our energy system that any government had ever given.\nI did not soften it then, and I will not soften it now.\nI told the nation that we had inherited a power grid and a generation system on life support, barely hanging on.\nBahamas Power and Light was carrying more than half a billion dollars of debt, and another hundred million in unfunded pension obligations. $600 million in total, on the shoulders of a company that could not pay for its own repairs.\nEngineers told us we needed $500 million in critical upgrades to prevent a complete failure of our electrical infrastructure.\nNot an inconvenience. A collapse.\nWe were running twenty-nine power stations across seventeen islands.\nDifferent manufacturers.\nDifferent eras.\nSome engines so old that no one still makes the parts to fix them.\nMore than sixty percent of the generation equipment in New Providence needed replacing.\nIn the Family Islands, that number was eighty percent.\nAnd to keep the lights on at all, we rented engines for over forty million dollars a year.\nRenting.\nMoney out the door, and not one dollar of it buying an asset we would own at the end.\nMy fellow Bahamians,\nThe system we inherited was not designed for the country we have become.\nSome of the equipment and cables were installed and laid down pre-independence, and a lot of it is more than a generation old.\nEvery government since has added to it.\nNo government before us took the bold position to rebuild a system that was made for the 21st century, taking into account rising temperatures, more violent and destructive hurricanes, and an economy that is growing faster than the time needed to complete the upgrades.\nBig changes are underway, the country is still quite a way from that reality – tonight I’m going to describe how we go from where we are now to a system that supports our families, businesses, and a better, bigger economy.\nThe Energy system is made up of two separate, but connected parts:\n- Generation and;\n- Transmission and Distribution\nTherefore, adding more generation does not, by itself, keep your lights on.\nGeneration is the engine.\nTransmission and distribution are the poles, the wires, the substations, the transformers and the cables that carry that power from the generators to your street and into your home.\nYou can have every engine in the fleet running perfectly, and if an overhead or underground cable fails or a transformer catches fire at a substation, the lights will go out.\nThat is what happened on the thirtieth of July.\nA transformer failure at the Blue Hills substation took the whole island down.\nIt was not a shortage of engines.\nAn underground cable fault caused the fire at a critical transformer.\nWith an overhead line fault, a crew can often see.\nWith a fault underground, they cannot.\nThey must trace it, section by section, street by street, sometimes by excavation. That is why that particular repair took days instead of hours.\nIt is not an excuse.\nIt is an explanation, because you are entitled to know why – although it does not ease the misery we all felt.\nThese challenges are compounded by the global heat wave we have been experiencing because of El Niño.\nThe demand on the system has broken our previous peak twice this season.\nHeat not only raises demand and increases the strain on the equipment; it accelerates its deterioration.\nWe are operating an old system in a new climate reality.\nAnd we do this on a chain of islands in the path of an increasingly violent hurricane season, in a world where energy supply chains are strained, and traditional short-term lead items have become long-term lead items and long-term lead items have become longer-term lead items as governments around the world race to upgrade their energy infrastructure.\nThis is our climate reality. This is our geographic reality.\nIt is not a complaint. It is the terrain on which we are building.\nAs I stated earlier, parts of the system were built before Independence.\nIt was built for a smaller population, a smaller economy, and a different climate.\nWe achieved political Independence more than fifty years ago. Now energy security must become part of our economic independence.\nI do not say this to point fingers.\nThe responsibility to act is ours.\nWe are not here to manage decline.\nWe are not here to defend the status quo.\nWe are transforming the whole system — generation, fuel, transmission, distribution, metering, finance, and governance.\nWhat we inherited is not what we will leave behind.\nNow, I could have managed Energy the way it has always been managed.\nRent another engine.\nPatch another cable.\nGet through one more summer and hand the problem to whoever comes next.\nThat is what was done for decades.\nI refused to do it.\nA crisis is an opportunity to fix what does not work.\nAnd we chose to fix it.\nFor the first time in our history, this country has a comprehensive, nationwide plan to modernise how we generate, store and distribute electricity across the whole archipelago. That plan is not a press release. It is under construction right now.\nWe separated generation from transmission and distribution, because that is how serious energy systems around the world are run.\nBahamas Grid Company was established to take on New Providence’s transmission and distribution, and it has delivered a $130 million foundational upgrade to the grid.\nThat work includes stronger steel poles, larger conductors, modern protection devices, fibre communications, a looped transmission system, and three new high-voltage substations.\nIn July, those substations had reached mechanical completion and entered final testing and commissioning.\nThat work produced measurable results.\nOutage frequency in New Providence fell forty-five percent against the historic average. Outage duration fell thirty-five percent, including during major weather events.\nOn ordinary days, close to half on both measures.\nSmart devices prevented tens of thousands of interruptions you never experienced, because they never happened.\nBahamas Grid Company is today a fully Bahamian-led utility, under Bahamian management, accountable to this country. That is as it should be.\nIn 2025, the foundational work was associated with a 45 percent reduction in outage frequency and a 35 percent reduction in outage duration compared with historical averages.\nThose gains are real.\nBut the outages Bahamians are experiencing now are also real.\nThe lesson is not that the earlier work failed.\nThe lesson is that the upgrade is not yet complete, that reliability has not improved evenly in every community, and that an old and overstressed system can still lose ground when demand rises, or critical equipment fails.\nThat is why our plan reaches beyond the grid.\nAt BPL, the work has been just as hard and far less visible. Generators that were unused or idle have been brought back.\nA rotor replaced.\nA major overhaul completed; turbochargers rebuilt.\nAlternators repaired.\nEngines returned to service one after another this year, each adding to the margin between you and a load-shedding schedule.\nWe executed a fuel hedge in December, two and a half million barrels, a full year of protection, so a war on the other side of the world does not impact your light bill the way it would have if we had not executed the hedge.\nAnd the Equity Rate Adjustment saved consumers $11.5 million last year.\nOne in five residential customers paid no base rate charge at all.\nWe signed agreements to bring liquefied natural gas into this country, a cleaner and cheaper fuel than the heavy oil we have burned for generations.\nWe have twenty-three microgrid and solar projects in development across the Family Islands, close to two hundred megawatts of new generation, with Bahamian companies as partners on almost every project.\nThe world has noticed.\nMoody’s upgraded our credit outlook twice in the span of one year, after not doing so in nearly two decades, and cited our energy reform as one of the major reasons.\nThat is how a crisis becomes an opportunity: we stop spending only to survive the next emergency and start investing to prevent the next emergency.\nBPL will continue restoring dependable generation while moving from rented, temporary units to permanent, efficient capacity.\nCritical overhauls must be completed.\nMaintenance must be planned from reliable asset data.\nBPL must integrate and expand battery storage systems.\nAnd rental generation must be phased out only when replacement capacity is tested and ready.\nBGC must complete and test the foundational grid work.\nThe new substations must be commissioned.\nThe transmission loop, switching capacity, protection systems, and control technology must be completed.\nWeak feeders must be rebuilt, and grid-readiness work must keep pace with new solar and generation projects.\nA power plant cannot help a customer if the grid cannot deliver the power.\nOn generation, capacity is returning now.\nBlue Hills GT2, out since the spring, is coming back. The battery storage system that shaves our evening peak returns to service.\nTwo Clifton Pier engines are scheduled to be back before the end of September.\nThe Blue Hills turbine under major inspection returns in the first quarter of next year.\nIn January we begin phasing out forty megawatts of rented generation, because permanent capacity will have replaced it.\nOn the network, the substation equipment, switchgear and high-voltage assets that failed this summer are being replaced, not patched.\nRemote monitoring goes live at Clifton Pier this year, and Blue Hills is next, so faults are diagnosed by data rather than discovered by darkness.\nOn fuel, the first phase of liquefied natural gas comes online this year, scaling to 190 megawatts of gas-fired capacity by late 2027, with new pipelines replacing a pipeline more than forty years old.\nThe combined cycle plants at Blue Hills will be commissioned during 2027 and 2028.\nWe will be able to produce power at a fraction of today’s fuel cost.\nMy fellow Bahamians, new engines, cables, transformers, solar and battery equipment alone will not save a company that is not run properly.\nAnd I will not stand here and ask you for patience while ignoring what needs fixing on the inside.\nLast year BPL spent approximately twenty million dollars on overtime despite the fact all transmission and distribution work is the responsibility of BGC.\nIn a single twelve-month period, three employees in one department received more than $600,000 in overtime between them.\nOne record shows an individual claiming eighteen hours on Christmas Day, then twenty-four hours each of the next three days that followed.\nNinety hours across four consecutive days.\nThe overwhelming majority of BPL’s workers are decent, skilled Bahamians who keep our lights on and go out in storms when the rest of us are told to stay inside.\nThey have my respect and my thanks. This is not about them.\nThis is about a system that permitted this type of abuse.\nA failure of management, of controls, of oversight, going back many years and across administrations.\nIt is not credible that a person works twenty-four hours straight, three days running, and does safe work.\nIt is not fair to the ratepayer, who funds every dollar.\nAnd it is not fair to the honest worker, whose profession is tarnished by it.\nA new overtime policy is in force.\nPlanned overtime is capped.\nAny technical worker whose overtime exceeds thirty percent of base salary triggers a review by the division head and the executive management team and is reported to the board.\nRepeat patterns are flagged.\nWe will apply this consistently and fairly, and we have engaged the unions, because their members deserve a properly run company as much as any customer does.\nEvery dollar recovered from waste is a dollar available for a spare part, a repair crew, new equipment and training.\nWe will put more information and more energy choices in the hands of customers.\nAdvanced meters can help identify outages faster and show households when and how they use electricity.\nWe will accelerate modern metering so that Bahamians can manage consumption instead of waiting until the end of the month to be surprised by a bill.\nAnd we will create greater flexibility for residential solar.\nIn consultation with URCA, BPL, consumer groups, and Bahamian solar installers, we will review the rules governing residential renewable-energy systems.\nOur objective is clear: simpler applications, predictable approval times, wider access to bi-directional meters, fair treatment of the energy a household produces, and appropriate credit for excess power supplied to the grid.\nMy fellow Bahamians, I am not going to stand here and tell you that every light will stay on tomorrow.\nWhat I will tell you is this.\nI know the scale of what we are dealing with. I have known it since the day I took office, and I have never once described it as smaller than it is.\nThere will be setbacks.\nThere will be equipment failures.\nThere may be temporary disruption as old infrastructure is removed, and new systems are connected, and as we build a more resilient system to survive and operate in the climate realities we face every year.\nBut difficulty is not a reason to retreat.\nIt is a reason to work harder, move faster, and communicate more honestly.\nI am not here to manage this.\nI am here to fix it.\nI said in Parliament that we will not accept the status quo; I meant it then, and I mean it now.\nRighting this ship requires more than engines and cable.\nIt requires a utility properly governed, staffed, financed, and held to account.\nIt requires management that answers for performance.\nThat work is under way.\nIt will not be comfortable, and it will not always be popular.\nBut it is the only route to a country where the power is stable, reliable and inexpensive.\nMy fellow Bahamians, you returned this Government to office, and you did not do so to keep things exactly as they were.\nYou gave us a mandate to build on progress and to confront the problems that others found too difficult or too costly to solve.\nYou elected us to build.\nTo build a grid that can carry the country we are becoming. To build generation we own instead of generation we rent. To build a utility that serves you rather than simply trying to survive. An energy system on which a young Bahamian can open a business, a mother can keep the fridge running, and an elder can sleep through a summer night.\nThis is the hardest infrastructure challenge our country has faced in fifty years of independence.\nIt sat for decades because leaving it was easier. We took it on because you deserve a government prepared to do the difficult thing rather than the convenient one.\nWe will finish what we started. Not because it will be easy, but because the alternative is another generation of Bahamians living in the dark and being told to wait.\nYou have waited long enough. What we inherited is not what we will leave behind.\nThank you. Good night.\nMay God bless you and the Commonwealth of The Bahamas.",
        "content": "My fellow Bahamians, good evening.\nIn October 2022, when I first spoke to you in detail about BPL, I did not leave the harsh reality of the state of our electricity infrastructure and the hundreds of millions needed to fix our aging systems to someone else.\nI came before you myself, because the buck stops with me.\nEver since, I have been honest about the scale of this challenge.\nIn April of 2025, ...",
