        "title": "‘Down the Arm of God’ Review: Caleb Landry Jones in a Sincere, if Heavy-Handed, Drama About a Texas Pastor Tackling Homelessness, and His Own Congregation",
        "link": "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-reviews/down-the-arm-of-god-review-caleb-landry-jones-1236675972/",
        "source": "The Hollywood Reporter",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.648822+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Down-the-Arm-of-God-film-still-6-EuropaCorp-H-2026.webp?w=969&h=545&crop=1",
        "full_content": "Caleb Landry Jones plays young, newly ordained pastor torn between the congregation he inherits in a small Texas town and an unhoused community desperately in need of help in Down the Arm of God, a powerful indie drama that’s clearly on the side of the angels.\nWith assistance from producer Luc Besson among others, Austrian-born director Peter Brunner, making his second feature with Jones after To the Night, elicits sincere, persuasive performances from his varied cast, including a fair few non-professionals with direct knowledge of homelessness. Peter Flinckenberg’s lively lensing and engagement with the distinctive light conditions of the location add an entrancing glow to the proceedings.\nDown the Arm of God\nCast: Caleb Landry Jones, India Marie Cross, Nick Stahl, Mena Suvari, John Jones, Whoopie Campfield, Dene Valentine, Amanda Davis, Atlas Fulton, Fable Fulton, Glenamzye Norment, Mike Johnson, Bobbie Lo Fruge, Crystal Salazar, Cassandra Fuentes, Kip Billups, Rhonda Bennett\nDirector: Peter Brunner\nScreenwriter: Peter Brunner, Caleb Landry Jones\n1 hour 46 minutes\nWhile all that weighs in the film’s favor, on the debit side Brunner and Jones make it pretty clear where their sympathies lie, which makes the film feel a bit manipulative and ploddingly polemical. Admittedly, viewers on the festival circuit, like those in Locarno where this debuted, and folks who might catch it on streamers or in niche distribution are unlikely to be very bothered about the film’s lack of impartiality. But arguably the script by Jones and director Brunner might have been a skoosh more impactful if the small-minded townsfolk, clearly coded as bigoted MAGA types, weren’t quite so hateful with only a few exceptions.\nAs the story starts, Landry Jones, himself a native Texan, is Nick, a fresh-faced minister from Dallas originally just settling into the small community near the Panhandle that he’s been assigned to shepherd. His flock consists mainly of white families whom we see mostly in their Sunday best, so it’s not easy to judge the economic realities that underpin this community, nor is the film very specific about what denomination exactly this is supposed to be. Suffice to say, that there’s no obvious dancing with snakes or speaking in tongues that suggest Pentecostalism, or choice of readings that would mark the congregation as, say, Episcopalian — although the choice of songs (like Sunday school banger “I Got the Joy Joy Joy”) Nick likes to play on the guitar lean low church.\nThis lack of specificity is also a bit of a flaw, because it suggests the filmmakers aren’t very interested in the theological forces and powers that be that shape what turns out to be a self-centered, possibly racist worldview. That narrow-mindedness is immediately apparent when Nick asks his congregation to help the group of unhoused people, a little over a dozen souls or so, who live in a makeshift camp on the edge of town. Most of them react fearfully and clearly want nothing to do with them, especially a classically WASP-ish blond-haired, clean-cut nuclear family led by Chris (Nick Stahl) and Anna (Mena Suvari), parents to pre-adolescent youngsters Elijah (Atlas Fulton) and Faye (Fable Fulton). Anna practically clutches her pearls when asked to help the homeless.\nThat said, some folks, like the local nurse (Amanda Davis), agree to come out to the encampment and treat the residents, many of whom are physically disabled, suffer from mental disorders or are war veterans probably still in the throes of PTSD. Young Raven (India Marie Cross, a real find making her feature debut here) emerges as a de facto leader of the unhoused, passionate about protecting her friends but able to see reason when Nick argues, for example, that they should come stay inside somewhere when a harsh winter storm damages their encampment.\nNaturally, the townsfolk fly into a rage at the mere suggestion that the unhoused should be put up in their community hall or school gymnasium, afeard as they are that their children will be in danger. Especially fierce in his objections is John (John Jones), a veteran himself of the Afghan war who lost his legs to a landmine. When confronted with fellow soldiers among the homeless community, John simply refuses to believe them when they say they too served in the military, or berates them for not taking up the benefits they might deserve. Angry words soon turn into protests and outright violence, with the gun violence that seems as inevitable as a winter storm on the horizon.\nAs Childish Gambino says, this is America, and at its best moments the script captures the way this nation’s irreconcilable differences play out in arguments with people interrupting and talking over one another, all sides refusing to listen. The climax is as needlessly tragic as one might expect, although Brunner rather rushes through the aftermath in a way that risks seeming implausible.\nBut at least there is a tiny sliver of hope in the very last scene, which features Jones’ real father in a silent but moving cameo role. Further balm is provided by the lovely, country-folk songs by Rhonda Bennett that she both plays onscreen here as one of the unhoused people and that are heard over the end credits.\nFull credits\nProduction companies: EuropaCorp, Roosevelt Film Lab\nCast: Caleb Landry Jones, India Marie Cross, Nick Stahl, Mena Suvari, John Jones, Whoopie Campfield, Dene Valentine, Amanda Davis, Atlas Fulton, Fable Fulton, Glenamzye Norment, Mike Johnson, Bobbie Lo Fruge, Crystal Salazar, Cassandra Fuentes, Kip Billups, Rhonda Bennett, Lacey Epperson, Ellis ‘Rusty’ Donelson, Monica Samora, Anfonnichio Jones, Kylea Broom, Casey Poque, James Piedra, Bart Guthrie, Penny Loudder, Patrick Jones\nDirector: Peter Brunner\nScreenwriter: Peter Brunner, Caleb Landry Jones\nProducers: Luc Besson, Caleb Landry Jones, Christian Sosa, Jon Wroblewski\nExecutive producers: Paul Merryman, Jennifer Kuczaj\nDirector of photography: Peter Flinckenberg\nProduction designer: Ashlie Adams\nCostume designer: Olivia Mori\nEditor: Peter Brunner\nSound editing: Clément Maurin\nSound mixing: Sean McCormick\nMusic: Tim Hecker\nMusic supervisor: Christian Chevalier\nCasting: Peter Brunner\nSales: Kinology\n1 hour 46 minutes\nTHR Newsletters\nSign up for THR news straight to your inbox every day",
        "content": "Caleb Landry Jones plays young, newly ordained pastor torn between the congregation he inherits in a small Texas town and an unhoused community desperately in need of help in Down the Arm of God, a powerful indie drama that’s clearly on the side of the angels.\nWith assistance from producer Luc Besson among others, Austrian-born director Peter Brunner, making his second feature with Jones after To ...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "7m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Inside the Fake Celebrity Podcast Scam Fooling Hollywood",
        "link": "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/celebrity-podcast-scam-bruce-vilanch-seth-rudetsky-1236676601/",
        "source": "The Hollywood Reporter",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-19T00:00:28.648819+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/GettyImages-2175385668-H-2024.jpg?w=1296&h=730&crop=1",
        "full_content": "When Seth Rudetsky received an invitation to appear on Nikki Glaser’s podcast, he had no particular reason to think it wasn’t real.\nRudetsky, the Broadway performer and SiriusXM host, gets asked to do podcasts all the time. Better yet, this one was offering him between $4,000 and $5,000 to appear. “Oh my God, they’re actually paying for a podcast,” he remembers thinking. A Glaser fan, Rudetsky quickly said yes.\nThe invitation also seemed to know an awful lot about him. It mentioned his Broadway cruise and his work with Sara Bareilles — the kind of details that suggested somebody had actually done their homework.\n“All these specific things about my career,” Rudetsky recalls. “I’m like, oh my God, they totally know my work.”\nBut they did not.\nRudetsky had stumbled into an increasingly sophisticated scam in which fraudsters impersonate famous podcasters and their representatives, using flattering, highly personalized invitations — most likely composed by AI — to lure prospective guests into handing over money, login credentials or control of social media accounts.\nAnd he is not the only recognizable Hollywood figure who fell for it. Bruce Vilanch believed Terry Gross wanted him on Fresh Air.\n“It was so articulate and so like her,” the veteran comedy writer and performer tells The Hollywood Reporter of an email he believed came from Gross. The correspondence that followed was equally persuasive, laying out the subjects the person identifying themselves as Gross intended to discuss with him, demonstrating what appeared to be deep, actual knowledge of Vilanch’s work.\n“I thought, ‘Well, this must really be Terry,'” Vilanch recalls.\nThere were, of course, red flags, in retrospect at least. Vilanch knew Fresh Air was an NPR production with an entire organization behind it, yet Gross seemed to be personally handling the booking. He decided perhaps she was simply “a maverick.”\nThen “Gross” asked him for money. The explanation was tailored to the moment: NPR was a nonprofit, they explained, but the government had cut funding and the organization needed help. Vilanch found the request strange, but by then the correspondence had established enough credibility that he decided to contribute.\n“It felt real,” he says. So he sent the money.\nBut the scammers didn’t disappear after getting his money. They continued preparing him for his Fresh Air appearance, sending more questions and ultimately scheduling the interview. When the appointed day arrived, Vilanch clicked the link. Three windows appeared on his screen: himself, one labeled NPR and a third containing a man.\n“Hello, this is Mike,” the man told him. “I will be conducting the interview.”\nVilanch stared at him in disbelief.\n“No, you won’t,” he replied. “I’m talking to Terry or I’m talking to nobody.”\nThe man explained that Gross was conducting another interview. Vilanch’s skepticism finally overwhelmed his willingness to believe. He waited about 15 minutes, then left. He sent one last message to his supposed host: “Whoever you are, I hope you enjoy the paltry sum that I sent.”\nVilanch then contacted WHYY, the Philadelphia public-radio station that produces Fresh Air, to warn them someone was impersonating Gross. To his surprise, they already knew.\nVilanch says the station asked him not to immediately post publicly about the scam and told him others had reported similar impersonations. He subsequently spoke with a WHYY media executive and turned over his entire email chain.\nFor Rudetsky, the scam had a different objective.\nAfter accepting the supposed Glaser invitation, he was asked to join her “manager” on Zoom for a technical setup involving Facebook Live. The man’s camera was off. He had a British accent but told Rudetsky he was in Pakistan. Even his email address struck Rudetsky and a colleague as suspiciously literal, something resembling “NikkiGlasersManager [at] Gmail.”\nYet each compounding oddity was just plausible enough to ignore. Eventually the “manager” began guiding Rudetsky through Facebook permissions. Administrative control of Rudetsky’s page still belonged to a marketing company he’d worked with years earlier, so Rudetsky brought its representative into the process.\nLater that night, Rudetsky tried to log into Facebook. He couldn’t. At first he blamed the marketing rep. Then, lying in bed around midnight, Rudetsky suddenly wondered whether something more sinister had happened. He Googled a combination of words: podcast, scam, celebrity, Facebook. Immediately, he says, he found an article describing essentially what had just happened to him.\n“Oh my God, oh my God,” he remembers thinking.\nSuddenly the oddities made sense: the email address, the manager overseas, the turned-off camera, the elaborate Facebook instructions. Rudetsky eventually regained control of his page. He says his former marketing company was also compromised, although Rudetsky doesn’t know precisely what the scammers accessed or what damage they caused.\nBoth Rudetsky and Vilanch were left wondering how people who considered themselves reasonably savvy had been taken in. And both kept returning to the same thing: the extraordinary specificity of the approaches.\n“I know why I fell for it too, because I’m so old. I don’t think about AI,” Rudetsky says.\nThe supposed Glaser representatives knew about his Broadway cruise and his work with Bareilles. To Rudetsky, those details functioned almost like authentication.\n“Wow,” he remembers thinking. “Nikki Glaser loves my Broadway cruise.”\nVilanch experienced much the same thing. The correspondence didn’t merely imitate the mechanics of a booking request. It seemed to imitate Terry Gross’ voice itself, including the preparation and literacy he associated with her.\n“They went above and beyond what they had to do to nail me,” he says. Then he reconsidered how much “work” might actually have been required. “Now I feel less bad because they could probably AI themselves into all of the work that I thought they had done.”\nThere appears to be no indication the scammer or scammers behind this web of deception are anywhere close to slowing down. In fact, Rudetsky’s experience did not even end with Nikki Glaser.\nNot long afterward, another invitation arrived, this time supposedly from Amy Poehler’s podcast. Rudetsky actually knows Poehler, so this time he employed the most reliable authentication technology available: He asked Amy Poehler. No, she replied, she hadn’t invited him onto her podcast.\nRudetsky says the fraudulent invitations continue to arrive. These days he sometimes entertains himself by engaging the scammers to see how far they’ll go, despite being warned that even replying may simply confirm that his email address belongs to an active target.\nAfter one supposed Poehler representative contacted him, Rudetsky eventually announced that he’d reported the scam. The response was remarkably cool: “Noted. We’ll see what happens next.”\nVilanch mostly remembers the embarrassment of realizing he’d been taken. “You just feel bad because you feel like a schmuck and because you’ve been duped,” he says.\nHearing of Rudetsky’s own troubles made him feel a little better.\n“They picked on two nice Jewish boys,” Vilanch says, “who give people the benefit of the doubt.”",
