        "image": "https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/img/article/2026-08/18/thumb/fitandfill/1200X628/1787073199-1578.jpg",
        "full_content": "India, US must address friction points, says Ambassador Sergio Gor\nGor said that the US embassy in India topped the list among US embassies worldwide in attracting over $20 billion in investment\nListen to This Article\nUS Ambassador Sergio Gor on Tuesday said India and the US must address friction points as true partners and work on predictable taxation and regulatory frameworks to allow business to thrive.\nSpeaking at the Indo-American Chamber of Commerce here, Gor stressed the need for candid, trust-based engagements on export controls and rock-solid intellectual property protections to give innovators confidence to invest, create and scale without fear.\nThe Indo-American Chamber of Commerce (IACC) held its national convention, themed Re-defining the India-US Comprehensive Strategic Partnership.\nGor said that the US embassy in India topped the list among US embassies worldwide in attracting over $20 billion in investment.\nWhen Indian companies invest in America, it is a win-win situation. They get access to one of the world's most dynamic markets, he said.\nAlso Read\n\"This relationship has unbelievable and incredible potential. To unleash the full power of the economic engine, we must address friction points together as true partners. We need predictable taxation and regulatory frameworks that allow business to thrive,\" he said.\nThe US ambassador, however, did not touch upon tariffs and visas, which have been India's major concerns.\nThe US envoy said when companies around the world tell him that their intellectual property rights have been stolen and they want to move their firms to India, they ask whether it is safe here and his answer is a resounding yes.\n\"We do trust India,\" he said, adding that President Donald Trump has trusted India and invested heavily in this relationship from day one.\nHe noted that in just over two decades, bilateral trade between India and the US has shattered records, skyrocketing from $20 billion to $240 billion in goods and services.\nThe 12-fold increase doesn't happen by accident, he said, asserting that the two countries share the ambition of taking this figure to $500 billion by 2030, which reflects the sheer scale of unmatched potential.\n\"When India and the US work together, they integrate value chains, accelerate breakthroughs in critical technologies and strengthen supply chain resilience,\" Gor said.\nMaharashtra Chief Minister Devendra Fadnavis, who was also at the event, said that technology will be the strongest part of the Indo-US relationship going forward.\nTechnology is changing the meaning of economic partnership, he said.\nFadnavis said that artificial intelligence, semiconductors, quantum technologies, cyber-security, cloud and data infrastructure, advanced manufacturing, medical technology, bio-technology and dual-use technologies constitute the core infrastructure of economic power in the next decade. And Maharashtra wants to participate in the entire spectrum.\n\"India has extraordinary engineering and scientific talent. The US has extraordinary capabilities in frontier research, technologies, capital and global enterprise. The next phase of cooperation between the two nations should be about joint innovation platforms, joint research, technology transfer, global capability centres, manufacturing partnerships and new global supply chains,\" the chief minister said.\n(Only the headline and picture of this report may have been reworked by the Business Standard staff; the rest of the content is auto-generated from a syndicated feed.)\nMore From This Section\nDon't miss the most important news and views of the day. Get them on our Telegram channel\nFirst Published: Aug 18 2026 | 10:43 PM IST",
